-- deferred-revenue-contract-liabilities-take-or-pay
UPDATE public.essays
   SET content = $c$<p><strong>In brief</strong></p><ul><li><p>Deferred revenue is a contract liability, an obligation to transfer goods or services for which the customer has already paid or for which payment is already due. It is recognised at the earlier of the two and released to revenue as each performance obligation is satisfied.</p></li><li><p>The amount in the liability depends on the five steps of PSAK 115. Allocation decides how much of a payment belongs to undelivered obligations, a significant financing component can make the liability grow with interest, and customer options and service warranties create obligations that must be deferred even when nothing was prepaid for them.</p></li><li><p>Rights that customers pay for but never use, such as unused make-up gas under a take-or-pay contract, are breakage. They become revenue in proportion to the rights customers do use or, when the seller does not expect to be entitled to breakage, only once use becomes remote.</p></li></ul><hr><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report deferred revenue, current maturities, of 6,012 at 31 December 2024 against 4,126 a year earlier, with a non-current portion of 13,118 against 7,017, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12; "Daftar" is the printed page). The current line is 0.10 percent of current liabilities (0.07 percent a year earlier), and the two lines together, 19,130, are 0.12 percent of total liabilities (0.07 percent a year earlier) and 0.06 percent of total assets.</p><p>After reading, a student should be able to say when a customer payment becomes a liability, measure it through the five steps, release it with journals, and recognise the same mechanics in airlines, telecoms and property developers.</p><p>All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration. The governing standard is PSAK 115 <em>Pendapatan dari Kontrak dengan Pelanggan</em> (formerly PSAK 72, IFRS 15), and PHE's notes use the new number.</p><h2>1. What deferred revenue is</h2><p>Cash received before performance is owed back in goods or services, so it is a liability. Kieso's older vocabulary calls it <em>unearned revenue</em> and lists it among current liabilities. Recorded the classic way, 10,000 received on 2 December for goods shipped FOB destination is credited to unearned revenue, and moves to sales revenue when the goods arrive on 5 December. Customer advances and deposits are kept apart. A returnable deposit is owed back in cash, while unearned revenue is owed in goods or services, a line that returns in section 8.</p><p>PSAK 115 keeps the idea but rebuilds the reasoning. Its <em>asset-liability approach</em> recognises revenue when a contract asset rises or a contract liability falls, rather than when revenue is "earned and realised". Three balances follow.</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Balance</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>What it is</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>When it arises</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Receivable</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>An unconditional right to consideration: only the passage of time is needed before payment is due (para. 108)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Performance done, or payment due under a non-cancellable contract</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Contract asset</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>A right to consideration conditional on something other than time, usually further performance (para. 107)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Performance done ahead of the right to bill</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Contract liability</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>An obligation to transfer goods or services for which consideration has been received or is due (para. 106)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Payment received or due ahead of performance</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The Trade receivables essay teaches the first two rows; this essay is about the third.</p><p>The contract liability is recognised "when the payment is made or the payment is due (whichever is earlier)" (para. 106). A payment that is due but unpaid creates the liability too, if the contract cannot be cancelled. For example (illustration), a PHE drilling subsidiary signs a non-cancellable contract on 1 October 2024 to drill one well and then monitor it for twelve months, for 3,000, invoiced on 1 November and payable on 1 December. On 1 December nothing has been paid and no drilling has started.</p><blockquote><p>Non-cancellable, payment due: Dr Trade receivables 3,000 / Cr Contract liability 3,000</p><p>Cancellable until drilling starts: no entry, because the right to payment is not yet unconditional</p></blockquote><p>Each contract is presented as one net position, a contract asset or a contract liability, never both (para. 105). PSAK 115 allows other names as long as receivables stay distinguishable (para. 109), which is why PHE and most Indonesian issuers still say <em>pendapatan tangguhan</em>, deferred revenue.</p><h2>2. The five steps and where the liability comes from</h2><p>Every contract liability is a product of the five steps of revenue recognition.</p><ol><li><p>Identify the contract with the customer.</p></li><li><p>Identify the <em>performance obligations</em>, the promises to transfer a distinct good or service.</p></li><li><p>Determine the <em>transaction price</em>, the consideration the entity expects to be entitled to.</p></li><li><p>Allocate the price to the obligations in proportion to their <em>standalone selling prices</em> (SSP), the prices at which each would sell on its own.</p></li><li><p>Recognise revenue when, or as, each obligation is satisfied by transferring control.</p></li></ol><p>The contract liability is the part of consideration received or due that step 4 has assigned to obligations step 5 has not yet satisfied. In the drilling contract, drilling and monitoring are distinct, since a customer could buy either from another contractor. Suppose drilling alone would sell for 2,700 and a year of monitoring for 900 (illustration).</p><blockquote><p>Total SSP = 2,700 + 900 = 3,600</p><p>Drilling = 3,000 × 2,700 / 3,600 = 2,250</p><p>Monitoring = 3,000 × 900 / 3,600 = 750</p></blockquote><p>The 600 bundle discount is spread across both obligations in proportion, unless there is evidence it belongs to one of them. The customer pays the 3,000 on 5 December. Drilling runs through December on the customer's well, so the customer controls the well as it is drilled and the obligation is satisfied over time; by 31 December the well is finished.</p><blockquote><p>5 December: Dr Cash 3,000 / Cr Trade receivables 3,000</p><p>31 December: Dr Contract liability 2,250 / Cr Revenue, drilling services 2,250</p></blockquote><p>The liability left at the year end is 750, all current because monitoring runs through 2025. Had the price been allocated by invoice line instead of SSP, with the contract billing 2,800 for drilling and 200 for monitoring, the year-end liability would be 200 and 550 of revenue would have been pulled into 2024. Step 4 exists to stop that.</p><h2>3. The transaction price: financing, refunds and rebates</h2><p>The transaction price is not always the cash received, and the differences flow into the liability.</p><p><strong>Significant financing component.</strong> When payment and performance are far apart, part of the payment is interest. If the customer pays early, the seller is borrowing from the customer, so the seller accretes interest on the contract liability and recognises more revenue than the cash received (paras. 60–65). The rate is the one a separate financing transaction between the two parties would carry, reflecting the credit of whoever receives the financing, here the seller (para. 64). For example (illustration), an industrial buyer prepays 10,000 on 1 January 2024 for gas a PHE subsidiary will deliver in full at the end of 2025, and the subsidiary could borrow for two years at 6 percent.</p><blockquote><p>Year 1 interest = 10,000 × 6% = 600; liability = 10,600</p><p>Year 2 interest = 10,600 × 6% = 636; liability = 11,236</p></blockquote><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Financing component recognised</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>No financing adjustment (if para. 62(a) or 63 applied)</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Receipt</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Cash 10,000 / Cr Contract liability 10,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Cash 10,000 / Cr Contract liability 10,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2024</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Interest expense 600 / Cr Contract liability 600</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>no entry</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2025</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Interest expense 636 / Cr Contract liability 636</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>no entry</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Delivery</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Contract liability 11,236 / Cr Revenue 11,236</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Dr Contract liability 10,000 / Cr Revenue 10,000</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Revenue less finance cost is 10,000 either way, but the left column shows 11,236 of revenue and 1,236 of finance cost, which is what a buyer paying the cash price at delivery would produce plus a separate loan. In this fact pattern, with a fixed delivery date two years out, neither exception applies, but they cover many common cases. No adjustment is needed when, at inception, the gap between payment and transfer is expected to be one year or less (para. 63), and there is no significant financing component when the customer prepaid and itself decides when the goods are transferred (para. 62(a)). The second exception is the one that applies to take-or-pay make-up gas in section 5.</p><p><strong>Refund liabilities.</strong> Consideration the seller expects to give back in cash is not transaction price at all. It is a <em>refund liability</em> (para. 55), a separate liability settled in cash rather than in goods. For example (illustration), a buyer pays 5,000 for gas and will receive a 2 percent volume rebate in cash, which the seller expects it to earn.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Cash 5,000 / Cr Revenue 4,900 / Cr Refund liability 100</p></blockquote><p>Only an obligation to deliver goods or services is a contract liability. The distinction comes back in section 8, because PHE's classification blurs it.</p><h2>4. Releasing the liability: point in time or over time</h2><p>The liability falls when control passes, and control passes in one of two ways. An obligation is satisfied <em>over time</em> if the customer receives and consumes the benefit as the entity performs, if the customer controls the asset as it is created, or if the asset has no alternative use to the seller and the seller has an enforceable right to payment for work done to date (para. 35). Otherwise it is satisfied <em>at a point in time</em>, judged by indicators such as a present right to payment, legal title, physical possession, transfer of risks and rewards, and the customer's acceptance (para. 38).</p><p>For over-time obligations the entity picks a <em>measure of progress</em>. <em>Output methods</em> count what has been delivered (months of service, metres drilled, BBTU of gas; one BBTU is a billion British thermal units). <em>Input methods</em> count effort, such as cost incurred against total expected cost, Kieso's cost-to-cost method. Monitoring is a stand-ready service delivered evenly, so time elapsed is the natural output measure.</p><blockquote><p>Monthly release = 750 / 12 = 62.5</p><p>Each month of 2025: Dr Contract liability 62.5 / Cr Revenue, monitoring services 62.5</p></blockquote><p>Gas delivered through a pipeline meter is released quantity by quantity. Whether that is a series of point-in-time sales or one obligation satisfied over time depends on whether the buyer consumes the gas as it arrives (IASB/FASB Transition Resource Group, July 2015). PHE's policy treats goods as generally transferred at a point in time and recognises crude and gas revenue "based on the actual lifting amount and sold to customers" (Note 3p, Daftar 5/62, PDF p. 79). Either way a prepayment for gas is released as the gas is delivered.</p><h2>5. Take-or-pay and breakage</h2><p>A take-or-pay clause makes the buyer pay for a minimum quantity whether or not it takes it. Such contracts typically soften it with a <em>make-up right</em>: the buyer may take the gas it paid for but did not take in later years, usually only after taking that year's own minimum. PHE's policy describes this balance: deferred revenue is "amounts billed and collected involving 'take or pay' gas quantities, which will be recognized as revenue when the related gas quantity are delivered to customers or when the contract expires" (Note 3u, Daftar 5/71, PDF p. 88).</p><p>Take a contract of the kind behind PHE's line (illustration). A subsidiary sells gas at USD 6 per MMBtu, which is 6 per BBTU in USD thousand. The annual contract quantity is 2,000 BBTU and the take-or-pay level is 80 percent, so 1,600 BBTU. Make-up gas may be taken in the two years after the shortfall.</p><blockquote><p>Year 1: buyer takes 1,200 BBTU; revenue = 1,200 × 6 = 7,200</p><p>Shortfall = 1,600 − 1,200 = 400 BBTU, billed and paid = 400 × 6 = 2,400</p><p>Dr Cash 2,400 / Cr Contract liability, take-or-pay 2,400</p></blockquote><p>The 2,400 is not revenue in year 1. The buyer has paid for gas it can still demand, so the seller owes 400 BBTU. The buyer decides when to take it, so para. 62(a) removes any financing component. Without a make-up right the shortfall payment would be revenue in year 1, because the seller would owe nothing more for it; the make-up right is what creates the liability.</p><blockquote><p>Year 2: buyer takes 1,750 BBTU; 1,600 is year 2's own minimum, paid in cash (9,600); 150 BBTU is make-up</p><p>Dr Contract liability 900 / Cr Revenue 900 (150 × 6)</p><p>Year 3: buyer takes exactly 1,600 BBTU; 250 BBTU of make-up remains unused when the right expires</p></blockquote><p>The unused 250 BBTU is <em>breakage</em>: a right the customer paid for and never exercised. PSAK 115 gives two treatments (paras. B44–B47). If the seller expects to be entitled to breakage, it recognises the expected amount in proportion to the pattern of rights the customer exercises. If it does not, it waits until the likelihood of the customer exercising its remaining rights becomes remote. The expected amount is subject to the constraint on variable consideration, so the seller must be confident it will not reverse. Suppose the seller's history shows that 40 percent of make-up gas is never taken (illustration).</p><blockquote><p>Expected make-up taken = 400 × 60% = 240 BBTU; expected breakage = 160 × 6 = 960</p><p>Year 2 share of expected use = 150 / 240 = 62.5%; breakage recognised = 960 × 62.5% = 600</p><p>Year 3 remainder at expiry = 2,400 − 900 − 600 = 900</p></blockquote><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Revenue from the 2,400</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Remote approach (PHE's policy)</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Proportional approach</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Year 2</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>900</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>900 + 600 = 1,500</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Year 3, at expiry</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,500</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>900</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Total</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>2,400</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>2,400</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Both release the same 2,400. The proportional approach brings breakage forward when the seller has evidence for it; the remote approach waits. PHE's wording, recognition "when the contract expires", fits the remote approach at its latest point and is not the proportional approach.</p><p>The current and non-current split follows the same schedule. A contract liability is current to the extent the seller expects to satisfy it within twelve months (PSAK 201.69, formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1), and it is settled by delivery or expiry, not cash. At the end of year 1, under the remote approach, the seller expects 150 BBTU of make-up in year 2 (900 current) and the rest to be taken or to expire later (1,500 non-current). A seller that must hand unclaimed amounts to a government records a liability for them, not breakage revenue (para. B47).</p><p>The shortfall payment would be a refund liability, not deferred revenue, if the contract obliged the seller to return cash for gas never taken. PHE's policy says the balance becomes revenue at expiry, so it reads as an obligation to deliver gas.</p><h2>6. Customer options, service warranties and upfront fees</h2><p>Some contract liabilities arise without any prepayment, because part of today's price pays for something promised later.</p><p><strong>Customer options.</strong> An option to buy more goods later is a separate performance obligation only if it gives a <em>material right</em>, something the customer would not get without the contract, such as a discount beyond what other customers receive (paras. B39–B40). For example (illustration), a buyer contracts for 1,000 BBTU at 6 (6,000) and receives an option to buy another 100 BBTU next year at 4.5, a discount not offered to anyone else. The seller expects 80 percent take-up.</p><blockquote><p>SSP of the option = (6.0 − 4.5) × 100 × 80% = 120</p><p>Gas = 6,000 × 6,000 / 6,120 = 5,882.35; option = 6,000 × 120 / 6,120 = 117.65</p><p>On delivery: Dr Cash 6,000 / Cr Revenue 5,882.35 / Cr Contract liability 117.65</p></blockquote><p>The 117.65 becomes revenue when the option is exercised or lapses.</p><p><strong>Warranties.</strong> There are two kinds. An <em>assurance-type</em> warranty promises only that the product works as agreed; it is not a performance obligation and its expected cost is a provision under PSAK 237 (see the Decommissioning provision essay for provisions). A <em>service-type</em> warranty, sold separately or covering more than agreed specifications, is a performance obligation. For example (illustration), a compressor package sells for 1,050 with a two-year extended service plan; SSPs are 1,000 and 100.</p><blockquote><p>Service plan = 1,050 × 100 / 1,100 = 95.45, held as a contract liability and released over two years</p></blockquote><p><strong>Upfront non-refundable fees.</strong> A connection fee, a joining fee or a franchise's initial fee usually pays in advance for the future goods or services, so it is deferred over the period they are supplied (paras. B48–B51). Franchise fees are the classic case.</p><h2>7. Deferred revenue across industries</h2><p><strong>Subscriptions and prepaid telecom.</strong> Prepaid mobile credit is a contract liability until the subscriber uses it, and expired credit is breakage. For example (illustration), an operator sells 1,000 of prepaid credit and its history says 8 percent will expire unused, so expected use is 920.</p><blockquote><p>When 460 has been used: revenue = 460 + 80 × (460 / 920) = 460 + 40 = 500; liability = 1,000 − 500 = 500</p></blockquote><p><strong>Airlines.</strong> Tickets sold for future flights sit in a liability, often called unearned transportation revenue, until the flight. Frequent-flyer miles awarded with a ticket are a material right. For example (illustration), a ticket sells for 300 and earns miles with an SSP of 15.</p><blockquote><p>Ticket = 300 × 300 / 315 = 285.71, released when flown; miles = 300 × 15 / 315 = 14.29, released when redeemed or when redemption becomes remote</p></blockquote><p><strong>Real estate pre-sales.</strong> Indonesian developers collect booking fees, down payments and instalments long before handover, so contract liabilities can be large on their balance sheets. Whether revenue comes at handover or over construction depends on para. 35(c): an apartment unit has no alternative use once sold, but revenue is over time only if the developer has an enforceable right to payment for work done to date, which turns on the sale agreement and local law. The gap between payment and handover is usually long enough for a significant financing component. For example (illustration), a buyer pays 1,000 three years before handover and the developer's borrowing rate is 8 percent.</p><blockquote><p>Liability at handover = 1,000 × 1.08³ = 1,259.71; revenue at handover 1,259.71; interest expense of 259.71 accreted on the liability over the three years</p></blockquote><p><strong>Construction and engineering.</strong> Billings ahead of progress give a contract liability, progress ahead of billings a contract asset, netted per contract (para. 105). The Trade receivables essay works the cost-to-cost example.</p><p><strong>Government grants are not contract liabilities.</strong> A grant related to an asset may be presented as <em>deferred income</em> under PSAK 220 (formerly PSAK 61, IAS 20), but the government is not a customer receiving goods or services, so PSAK 115 does not apply.</p><h2>8. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Note 24</h2><p>PHE splits the balance into take-or-pay gas and others, each with a current and non-current portion (Note 24, Daftar 5/118, PDF p. 135).</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2023</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Take-or-pay gas, current</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>4,718</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,332</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Others, current</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,294</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2,794</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Current portion</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>6,012</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>4,126</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Take-or-pay gas, non-current</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>10,800</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,898</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Others, non-current</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2,318</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,119</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Non-current portion</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>13,118</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>7,017</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Total deferred revenue</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>19,130</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>11,143</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>Growth in total = 19,130 / 11,143 − 1 = 71.7%</p><p>Take-or-pay gas = 4,718 + 10,800 = 15,518 (2024); 1,332 + 3,898 = 5,230 (2023), 2.97 times the 2023 balance</p><p>Current share = 6,012 / 19,130 = 31.4%</p><p>Total deferred revenue / revenue = 19,130 / 14,330,340 = 0.13%</p></blockquote><p>The take-or-pay balance rose by 15,518 − 5,230 = 10,288; at section 5's illustrative 6 per BBTU that would be about 1,715 BBTU of unrecovered shortfall. In the mechanics of section 5 the rise is consistent with buyers taking less than their minimum quantities in 2024, while "others" fell. Two-thirds sits in non-current, consistent with make-up gas, or expiry, expected after 2025.</p><p>Note 24 gives the balances at both year-ends, which meets the first part of para. 116. It does not give the revenue recognised from the opening balance (para. 116(b)) or explain the change (para. 118). Para. 120's remaining-obligation disclosure can be skipped for sales billed per unit delivered (para. 121(b)). At 0.13 percent of revenue the omissions are immaterial under PSAK 201, but they are why a reader cannot tell how much of the 11,143 at the start of 2024 became revenue, how much was added, or how much is breakage at expiry.</p><p>PHE also presents deferred revenue as a financial liability. Its policy lists it among liabilities classified as loans and borrowings (Note 3, Daftar 5/44, PDF p. 61), a label from PSAK 55, which PSAK 109 replaced; it appears among other financial liabilities in Note 47 (Daftar 5/222, PDF p. 239); and the PSAK 107 liquidity table shows the 19,130 as a contractual outflow, 6,012 within one year and 13,118 in one to five years (Daftar 5/233, PDF p. 250). Under PSAK 232 (formerly PSAK 50, IAS 32), deferred revenue is not a financial liability because it is settled by delivering goods or services, not cash (application guidance AG11). That is the line between deposits and unearned revenue drawn in section 1. Since PHE's own policy settles the take-or-pay balance in gas or at expiry, the classification is hard to reconcile with it, though in a line this small it is a presentation inconsistency rather than a material misstatement.</p><p>Note 43e (Daftar 5/155–5/156, PDF pp. 172–173) carries deferred tax on deferred revenue of 2,923 at the end of 2023 (2,884 in the asset schedule, 39 as a deductible item in the liability schedule) and 2,874 at the end of 2024, almost all moved from one schedule to the other (net charge 49). That is 15 percent of the 2024 balance, below any PSC tax rate, so not all of the balance appears to be a temporary difference (see the Deferred tax assets essay).</p><h2>9. Upstream note</h2><p>Take-or-pay protects the investment case for gas fields, whose output must flow to a contracted buyer through dedicated infrastructure. Under a PSC, a gas sales agreement covers both the contractor's and the government's share of the gas. PHE's FS state the policy and the balance but not the contract terms, the make-up period, or whether shortfall payments on the government's share sit in PHE's balance.</p><h2>10. Related lines</h2><ul><li><p><code>deferred-revenue-non-current</code>: the same balance, expected to be released after twelve months.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/revenues">revenues</a>: where the liability is released.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/trade-receivables-net">trade-receivables-net</a>: the unconditional right that a due-date contract liability creates, and contract assets.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/trade-payables">trade-payables</a>: the boundary between obligations settled in cash and those settled in goods.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/decommissioning-site-restoration-provision">decommissioning-site-restoration-provision</a>: where assurance warranties and other provisions belong.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets">deferred-tax-assets</a> and <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets">deferred-tax-liabilities</a>: the tax effect in Note 43e.</p></li></ul><h3>Least-confident assertions</h3><ul><li><p>The reading that PHE's take-or-pay balance follows the remote approach to breakage rests on the phrase "or when the contract expires" in Note 3u. PHE does not state its breakage method, and the contract terms (make-up period, refund clauses) are not disclosed.</p></li><li><p>Classifying deferred revenue as a financial liability conflicts with IAS 32 AG11 only if no part of the balance is refundable in cash. PHE's policy implies it is not, but the "others" component (3,612 at the end of 2024) is not described at all.</p></li><li><p>The reading of the 2024 increase as larger buyer shortfalls follows from the mechanics, and the 1,715 BBTU uses an illustrative price. A new contract with a larger minimum, or a reclassification, would produce the same movement, and PHE gives no roll-forward.</p></li><li><p>That the Note 43e tax balance on deferred revenue implies only part of the balance is a temporary difference is an inference. Different tax rates across PSCs, or balances held in entities that recognise no deferred tax, could also explain it.</p></li><li><p>Applying para. 62(a) to make-up gas assumes the buyer controls the timing of make-up. Contracts that fix a make-up schedule could carry a financing component.</p></li></ul><h2>References</h2><ol><li><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12); Note 3 financial liabilities (Daftar 5/44, PDF p. 61); Note 3p (Daftar 5/61–5/63, PDF pp. 78–80); Note 3u (Daftar 5/71, PDF p. 88); Note 24 (Daftar 5/118, PDF p. 135); Note 35 (Daftar 5/148, PDF p. 165); Note 43e (Daftar 5/155–5/156, PDF pp. 172–173); financial instruments by category (Daftar 5/222, PDF p. 239); liquidity table (Daftar 5/233, PDF p. 250). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 115 <em>Pendapatan dari Kontrak dengan Pelanggan</em> (IFRS 15). Paras. 35, 38, 55, 62–64, 105–109, 116, 118, 120–121, B40, B42, B44–B49 VERIFIED (web, IFRS 15 as adopted, Commission Regulation (EU) 2016/1905, legislation.gov.uk). Paras. 60–61, B39, B50–B51 STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED. IASB/FASB Transition Resource Group, July 2015, Agenda Paper 43 <em>Commodities</em> (point in time vs over time for commodity deliveries). VERIFIED (web, ifrs.org; PwC Viewpoint TRG summary).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 201 (IAS 1), para. 69 (current liabilities) and materiality. Number VERIFIED (web, IAI renumbering list); content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 232 <em>Instrumen Keuangan: Penyajian</em> (IAS 32), AG11 (deferred revenue is not a financial liability). VERIFIED (web, NWB reproduction of IAS 32 AL11; HMRC Corporate Finance Manual CFM21130, secondary).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 237 (IAS 37), PSAK 220 (IAS 20), PSAK 107 (IFRS 7), PSAK 55 (IAS 39, superseded by PSAK 109): numbers VERIFIED (web, IAI renumbering list, as in sibling essays); content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, <em>Intermediate Accounting</em>, IFRS edition, chs. 13 and 18. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.</p></li></ol>$c$,
       content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In brief","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Deferred revenue is a contract liability, an obligation to transfer goods or services for which the customer has already paid or for which payment is already due. It is recognised at the earlier of the two and released to revenue as each performance obligation is satisfied.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The amount in the liability depends on the five steps of PSAK 115. Allocation decides how much of a payment belongs to undelivered obligations, a significant financing component can make the liability grow with interest, and customer options and service warranties create obligations that must be deferred even when nothing was prepaid for them.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Rights that customers pay for but never use, such as unused make-up gas under a take-or-pay contract, are breakage. They become revenue in proportion to the rights customers do use or, when the seller does not expect to be entitled to breakage, only once use becomes remote.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report deferred revenue, current maturities, of 6,012 at 31 December 2024 against 4,126 a year earlier, with a non-current portion of 13,118 against 7,017, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12; \"Daftar\" is the printed page). The current line is 0.10 percent of current liabilities (0.07 percent a year earlier), and the two lines together, 19,130, are 0.12 percent of total liabilities (0.07 percent a year earlier) and 0.06 percent of total assets.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"After reading, a student should be able to say when a customer payment becomes a liability, measure it through the five steps, release it with journals, and recognise the same mechanics in airlines, telecoms and property developers.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration. The governing standard is PSAK 115 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Pendapatan dari Kontrak dengan Pelanggan","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 72, IFRS 15), and PHE's notes use the new number.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1. What deferred revenue is","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash received before performance is owed back in goods or services, so it is a liability. Kieso's older vocabulary calls it ","type":"text"},{"text":"unearned revenue","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" and lists it among current liabilities. Recorded the classic way, 10,000 received on 2 December for goods shipped FOB destination is credited to unearned revenue, and moves to sales revenue when the goods arrive on 5 December. Customer advances and deposits are kept apart. A returnable deposit is owed back in cash, while unearned revenue is owed in goods or services, a line that returns in section 8.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 115 keeps the idea but rebuilds the reasoning. Its ","type":"text"},{"text":"asset-liability approach","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" recognises revenue when a contract asset rises or a contract liability falls, rather than when revenue is \"earned and realised\". Three balances follow.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Balance","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"What it is","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"When it arises","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Receivable","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"An unconditional right to consideration: only the passage of time is needed before payment is due (para. 108)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Performance done, or payment due under a non-cancellable contract","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Contract asset","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A right to consideration conditional on something other than time, usually further performance (para. 107)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Performance done ahead of the right to bill","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Contract liability","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"An obligation to transfer goods or services for which consideration has been received or is due (para. 106)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Payment received or due ahead of performance","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The Trade receivables essay teaches the first two rows; this essay is about the third.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The contract liability is recognised \"when the payment is made or the payment is due (whichever is earlier)\" (para. 106). A payment that is due but unpaid creates the liability too, if the contract cannot be cancelled. For example (illustration), a PHE drilling subsidiary signs a non-cancellable contract on 1 October 2024 to drill one well and then monitor it for twelve months, for 3,000, invoiced on 1 November and payable on 1 December. On 1 December nothing has been paid and no drilling has started.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Non-cancellable, payment due: Dr Trade receivables 3,000 / Cr Contract liability 3,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cancellable until drilling starts: no entry, because the right to payment is not yet unconditional","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Each contract is presented as one net position, a contract asset or a contract liability, never both (para. 105). PSAK 115 allows other names as long as receivables stay distinguishable (para. 109), which is why PHE and most Indonesian issuers still say ","type":"text"},{"text":"pendapatan tangguhan","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", deferred revenue.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2. The five steps and where the liability comes from","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Every contract liability is a product of the five steps of revenue recognition.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Identify the contract with the customer.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Identify the ","type":"text"},{"text":"performance obligations","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", the promises to transfer a distinct good or service.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Determine the ","type":"text"},{"text":"transaction price","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", the consideration the entity expects to be entitled to.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Allocate the price to the obligations in proportion to their ","type":"text"},{"text":"standalone selling prices","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (SSP), the prices at which each would sell on its own.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Recognise revenue when, or as, each obligation is satisfied by transferring control.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The contract liability is the part of consideration received or due that step 4 has assigned to obligations step 5 has not yet satisfied. In the drilling contract, drilling and monitoring are distinct, since a customer could buy either from another contractor. Suppose drilling alone would sell for 2,700 and a year of monitoring for 900 (illustration).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total SSP = 2,700 + 900 = 3,600","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Drilling = 3,000 × 2,700 / 3,600 = 2,250","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Monitoring = 3,000 × 900 / 3,600 = 750","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The 600 bundle discount is spread across both obligations in proportion, unless there is evidence it belongs to one of them. The customer pays the 3,000 on 5 December. Drilling runs through December on the customer's well, so the customer controls the well as it is drilled and the obligation is satisfied over time; by 31 December the well is finished.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5 December: Dr Cash 3,000 / Cr Trade receivables 3,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 December: Dr Contract liability 2,250 / Cr Revenue, drilling services 2,250","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The liability left at the year end is 750, all current because monitoring runs through 2025. Had the price been allocated by invoice line instead of SSP, with the contract billing 2,800 for drilling and 200 for monitoring, the year-end liability would be 200 and 550 of revenue would have been pulled into 2024. Step 4 exists to stop that.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3. The transaction price: financing, refunds and rebates","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The transaction price is not always the cash received, and the differences flow into the liability.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Significant financing component.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" When payment and performance are far apart, part of the payment is interest. If the customer pays early, the seller is borrowing from the customer, so the seller accretes interest on the contract liability and recognises more revenue than the cash received (paras. 60–65). The rate is the one a separate financing transaction between the two parties would carry, reflecting the credit of whoever receives the financing, here the seller (para. 64). For example (illustration), an industrial buyer prepays 10,000 on 1 January 2024 for gas a PHE subsidiary will deliver in full at the end of 2025, and the subsidiary could borrow for two years at 6 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year 1 interest = 10,000 × 6% = 600; liability = 10,600","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year 2 interest = 10,600 × 6% = 636; liability = 11,236","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Financing component recognised","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"No financing adjustment (if para. 62(a) or 63 applied)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Receipt","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 10,000 / Cr Contract liability 10,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 10,000 / Cr Contract liability 10,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Interest expense 600 / Cr Contract liability 600","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"no entry","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2025","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Interest expense 636 / Cr Contract liability 636","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"no entry","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Delivery","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Contract liability 11,236 / Cr Revenue 11,236","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Contract liability 10,000 / Cr Revenue 10,000","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Revenue less finance cost is 10,000 either way, but the left column shows 11,236 of revenue and 1,236 of finance cost, which is what a buyer paying the cash price at delivery would produce plus a separate loan. In this fact pattern, with a fixed delivery date two years out, neither exception applies, but they cover many common cases. No adjustment is needed when, at inception, the gap between payment and transfer is expected to be one year or less (para. 63), and there is no significant financing component when the customer prepaid and itself decides when the goods are transferred (para. 62(a)). The second exception is the one that applies to take-or-pay make-up gas in section 5.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Refund liabilities.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Consideration the seller expects to give back in cash is not transaction price at all. It is a ","type":"text"},{"text":"refund liability","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (para. 55), a separate liability settled in cash rather than in goods. For example (illustration), a buyer pays 5,000 for gas and will receive a 2 percent volume rebate in cash, which the seller expects it to earn.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 5,000 / Cr Revenue 4,900 / Cr Refund liability 100","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Only an obligation to deliver goods or services is a contract liability. The distinction comes back in section 8, because PHE's classification blurs it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4. Releasing the liability: point in time or over time","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The liability falls when control passes, and control passes in one of two ways. An obligation is satisfied ","type":"text"},{"text":"over time","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" if the customer receives and consumes the benefit as the entity performs, if the customer controls the asset as it is created, or if the asset has no alternative use to the seller and the seller has an enforceable right to payment for work done to date (para. 35). Otherwise it is satisfied ","type":"text"},{"text":"at a point in time","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", judged by indicators such as a present right to payment, legal title, physical possession, transfer of risks and rewards, and the customer's acceptance (para. 38).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For over-time obligations the entity picks a ","type":"text"},{"text":"measure of progress","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":". ","type":"text"},{"text":"Output methods","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" count what has been delivered (months of service, metres drilled, BBTU of gas; one BBTU is a billion British thermal units). ","type":"text"},{"text":"Input methods","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" count effort, such as cost incurred against total expected cost, Kieso's cost-to-cost method. Monitoring is a stand-ready service delivered evenly, so time elapsed is the natural output measure.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Monthly release = 750 / 12 = 62.5","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Each month of 2025: Dr Contract liability 62.5 / Cr Revenue, monitoring services 62.5","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Gas delivered through a pipeline meter is released quantity by quantity. Whether that is a series of point-in-time sales or one obligation satisfied over time depends on whether the buyer consumes the gas as it arrives (IASB/FASB Transition Resource Group, July 2015). PHE's policy treats goods as generally transferred at a point in time and recognises crude and gas revenue \"based on the actual lifting amount and sold to customers\" (Note 3p, Daftar 5/62, PDF p. 79). Either way a prepayment for gas is released as the gas is delivered.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5. Take-or-pay and breakage","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A take-or-pay clause makes the buyer pay for a minimum quantity whether or not it takes it. Such contracts typically soften it with a ","type":"text"},{"text":"make-up right","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": the buyer may take the gas it paid for but did not take in later years, usually only after taking that year's own minimum. PHE's policy describes this balance: deferred revenue is \"amounts billed and collected involving 'take or pay' gas quantities, which will be recognized as revenue when the related gas quantity are delivered to customers or when the contract expires\" (Note 3u, Daftar 5/71, PDF p. 88).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Take a contract of the kind behind PHE's line (illustration). A subsidiary sells gas at USD 6 per MMBtu, which is 6 per BBTU in USD thousand. The annual contract quantity is 2,000 BBTU and the take-or-pay level is 80 percent, so 1,600 BBTU. Make-up gas may be taken in the two years after the shortfall.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year 1: buyer takes 1,200 BBTU; revenue = 1,200 × 6 = 7,200","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Shortfall = 1,600 − 1,200 = 400 BBTU, billed and paid = 400 × 6 = 2,400","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Cash 2,400 / Cr Contract liability, take-or-pay 2,400","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The 2,400 is not revenue in year 1. The buyer has paid for gas it can still demand, so the seller owes 400 BBTU. The buyer decides when to take it, so para. 62(a) removes any financing component. Without a make-up right the shortfall payment would be revenue in year 1, because the seller would owe nothing more for it; the make-up right is what creates the liability.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year 2: buyer takes 1,750 BBTU; 1,600 is year 2's own minimum, paid in cash (9,600); 150 BBTU is make-up","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Contract liability 900 / Cr Revenue 900 (150 × 6)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year 3: buyer takes exactly 1,600 BBTU; 250 BBTU of make-up remains unused when the right expires","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The unused 250 BBTU is ","type":"text"},{"text":"breakage","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": a right the customer paid for and never exercised. PSAK 115 gives two treatments (paras. B44–B47). If the seller expects to be entitled to breakage, it recognises the expected amount in proportion to the pattern of rights the customer exercises. If it does not, it waits until the likelihood of the customer exercising its remaining rights becomes remote. The expected amount is subject to the constraint on variable consideration, so the seller must be confident it will not reverse. Suppose the seller's history shows that 40 percent of make-up gas is never taken (illustration).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Expected make-up taken = 400 × 60% = 240 BBTU; expected breakage = 160 × 6 = 960","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year 2 share of expected use = 150 / 240 = 62.5%; breakage recognised = 960 × 62.5% = 600","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year 3 remainder at expiry = 2,400 − 900 − 600 = 900","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Revenue from the 2,400","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Remote approach (PHE's policy)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Proportional approach","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year 2","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"900","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"900 + 600 = 1,500","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year 3, at expiry","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,500","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"900","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,400","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,400","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Both release the same 2,400. The proportional approach brings breakage forward when the seller has evidence for it; the remote approach waits. PHE's wording, recognition \"when the contract expires\", fits the remote approach at its latest point and is not the proportional approach.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The current and non-current split follows the same schedule. A contract liability is current to the extent the seller expects to satisfy it within twelve months (PSAK 201.69, formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1), and it is settled by delivery or expiry, not cash. At the end of year 1, under the remote approach, the seller expects 150 BBTU of make-up in year 2 (900 current) and the rest to be taken or to expire later (1,500 non-current). A seller that must hand unclaimed amounts to a government records a liability for them, not breakage revenue (para. B47).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The shortfall payment would be a refund liability, not deferred revenue, if the contract obliged the seller to return cash for gas never taken. PHE's policy says the balance becomes revenue at expiry, so it reads as an obligation to deliver gas.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6. Customer options, service warranties and upfront fees","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Some contract liabilities arise without any prepayment, because part of today's price pays for something promised later.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Customer options.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" An option to buy more goods later is a separate performance obligation only if it gives a ","type":"text"},{"text":"material right","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", something the customer would not get without the contract, such as a discount beyond what other customers receive (paras. B39–B40). For example (illustration), a buyer contracts for 1,000 BBTU at 6 (6,000) and receives an option to buy another 100 BBTU next year at 4.5, a discount not offered to anyone else. The seller expects 80 percent take-up.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"SSP of the option = (6.0 − 4.5) × 100 × 80% = 120","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Gas = 6,000 × 6,000 / 6,120 = 5,882.35; option = 6,000 × 120 / 6,120 = 117.65","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"On delivery: Dr Cash 6,000 / Cr Revenue 5,882.35 / Cr Contract liability 117.65","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The 117.65 becomes revenue when the option is exercised or lapses.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Warranties.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" There are two kinds. An ","type":"text"},{"text":"assurance-type","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" warranty promises only that the product works as agreed; it is not a performance obligation and its expected cost is a provision under PSAK 237 (see the Decommissioning provision essay for provisions). A ","type":"text"},{"text":"service-type","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" warranty, sold separately or covering more than agreed specifications, is a performance obligation. For example (illustration), a compressor package sells for 1,050 with a two-year extended service plan; SSPs are 1,000 and 100.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Service plan = 1,050 × 100 / 1,100 = 95.45, held as a contract liability and released over two years","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Upfront non-refundable fees.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A connection fee, a joining fee or a franchise's initial fee usually pays in advance for the future goods or services, so it is deferred over the period they are supplied (paras. B48–B51). Franchise fees are the classic case.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7. Deferred revenue across industries","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Subscriptions and prepaid telecom.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Prepaid mobile credit is a contract liability until the subscriber uses it, and expired credit is breakage. For example (illustration), an operator sells 1,000 of prepaid credit and its history says 8 percent will expire unused, so expected use is 920.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"When 460 has been used: revenue = 460 + 80 × (460 / 920) = 460 + 40 = 500; liability = 1,000 − 500 = 500","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Airlines.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Tickets sold for future flights sit in a liability, often called unearned transportation revenue, until the flight. Frequent-flyer miles awarded with a ticket are a material right. For example (illustration), a ticket sells for 300 and earns miles with an SSP of 15.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Ticket = 300 × 300 / 315 = 285.71, released when flown; miles = 300 × 15 / 315 = 14.29, released when redeemed or when redemption becomes remote","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Real estate pre-sales.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Indonesian developers collect booking fees, down payments and instalments long before handover, so contract liabilities can be large on their balance sheets. Whether revenue comes at handover or over construction depends on para. 35(c): an apartment unit has no alternative use once sold, but revenue is over time only if the developer has an enforceable right to payment for work done to date, which turns on the sale agreement and local law. The gap between payment and handover is usually long enough for a significant financing component. For example (illustration), a buyer pays 1,000 three years before handover and the developer's borrowing rate is 8 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Liability at handover = 1,000 × 1.08³ = 1,259.71; revenue at handover 1,259.71; interest expense of 259.71 accreted on the liability over the three years","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Construction and engineering.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Billings ahead of progress give a contract liability, progress ahead of billings a contract asset, netted per contract (para. 105). The Trade receivables essay works the cost-to-cost example.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Government grants are not contract liabilities.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A grant related to an asset may be presented as ","type":"text"},{"text":"deferred income","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" under PSAK 220 (formerly PSAK 61, IAS 20), but the government is not a customer receiving goods or services, so PSAK 115 does not apply.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Note 24","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE splits the balance into take-or-pay gas and others, each with a current and non-current portion (Note 24, Daftar 5/118, PDF p. 135).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2023","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Take-or-pay gas, current","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4,718","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,332","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Others, current","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,294","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,794","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Current portion","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6,012","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4,126","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Take-or-pay gas, non-current","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10,800","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,898","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Others, non-current","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,318","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,119","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Non-current portion","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"13,118","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7,017","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total deferred revenue","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"19,130","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"11,143","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Growth in total = 19,130 / 11,143 − 1 = 71.7%","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Take-or-pay gas = 4,718 + 10,800 = 15,518 (2024); 1,332 + 3,898 = 5,230 (2023), 2.97 times the 2023 balance","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Current share = 6,012 / 19,130 = 31.4%","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total deferred revenue / revenue = 19,130 / 14,330,340 = 0.13%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The take-or-pay balance rose by 15,518 − 5,230 = 10,288; at section 5's illustrative 6 per BBTU that would be about 1,715 BBTU of unrecovered shortfall. In the mechanics of section 5 the rise is consistent with buyers taking less than their minimum quantities in 2024, while \"others\" fell. Two-thirds sits in non-current, consistent with make-up gas, or expiry, expected after 2025.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Note 24 gives the balances at both year-ends, which meets the first part of para. 116. It does not give the revenue recognised from the opening balance (para. 116(b)) or explain the change (para. 118). Para. 120's remaining-obligation disclosure can be skipped for sales billed per unit delivered (para. 121(b)). At 0.13 percent of revenue the omissions are immaterial under PSAK 201, but they are why a reader cannot tell how much of the 11,143 at the start of 2024 became revenue, how much was added, or how much is breakage at expiry.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE also presents deferred revenue as a financial liability. Its policy lists it among liabilities classified as loans and borrowings (Note 3, Daftar 5/44, PDF p. 61), a label from PSAK 55, which PSAK 109 replaced; it appears among other financial liabilities in Note 47 (Daftar 5/222, PDF p. 239); and the PSAK 107 liquidity table shows the 19,130 as a contractual outflow, 6,012 within one year and 13,118 in one to five years (Daftar 5/233, PDF p. 250). Under PSAK 232 (formerly PSAK 50, IAS 32), deferred revenue is not a financial liability because it is settled by delivering goods or services, not cash (application guidance AG11). That is the line between deposits and unearned revenue drawn in section 1. Since PHE's own policy settles the take-or-pay balance in gas or at expiry, the classification is hard to reconcile with it, though in a line this small it is a presentation inconsistency rather than a material misstatement.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Note 43e (Daftar 5/155–5/156, PDF pp. 172–173) carries deferred tax on deferred revenue of 2,923 at the end of 2023 (2,884 in the asset schedule, 39 as a deductible item in the liability schedule) and 2,874 at the end of 2024, almost all moved from one schedule to the other (net charge 49). That is 15 percent of the 2024 balance, below any PSC tax rate, so not all of the balance appears to be a temporary difference (see the Deferred tax assets essay).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9. Upstream note","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Take-or-pay protects the investment case for gas fields, whose output must flow to a contracted buyer through dedicated infrastructure. Under a PSC, a gas sales agreement covers both the contractor's and the government's share of the gas. PHE's FS state the policy and the balance but not the contract terms, the make-up period, or whether shortfall payments on the government's share sit in PHE's balance.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10. Related lines","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"deferred-revenue-non-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"text":": the same balance, expected to be released after twelve months.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"revenues","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/revenues","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": where the liability is released.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"trade-receivables-net","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/trade-receivables-net","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the unconditional right that a due-date contract liability creates, and contract assets.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"trade-payables","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/trade-payables","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the boundary between obligations settled in cash and those settled in goods.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"decommissioning-site-restoration-provision","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/decommissioning-site-restoration-provision","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": where assurance warranties and other provisions belong.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"deferred-tax-assets","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"text":"deferred-tax-liabilities","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the tax effect in Note 43e.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Least-confident assertions","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The reading that PHE's take-or-pay balance follows the remote approach to breakage rests on the phrase \"or when the contract expires\" in Note 3u. PHE does not state its breakage method, and the contract terms (make-up period, refund clauses) are not disclosed.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Classifying deferred revenue as a financial liability conflicts with IAS 32 AG11 only if no part of the balance is refundable in cash. PHE's policy implies it is not, but the \"others\" component (3,612 at the end of 2024) is not described at all.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The reading of the 2024 increase as larger buyer shortfalls follows from the mechanics, and the 1,715 BBTU uses an illustrative price. A new contract with a larger minimum, or a reclassification, would produce the same movement, and PHE gives no roll-forward.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"That the Note 43e tax balance on deferred revenue implies only part of the balance is a temporary difference is an inference. Different tax rates across PSCs, or balances held in entities that recognise no deferred tax, could also explain it.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Applying para. 62(a) to make-up gas assumes the buyer controls the timing of make-up. Contracts that fix a make-up schedule could carry a financing component.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"References","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12); Note 3 financial liabilities (Daftar 5/44, PDF p. 61); Note 3p (Daftar 5/61–5/63, PDF pp. 78–80); Note 3u (Daftar 5/71, PDF p. 88); Note 24 (Daftar 5/118, PDF p. 135); Note 35 (Daftar 5/148, PDF p. 165); Note 43e (Daftar 5/155–5/156, PDF pp. 172–173); financial instruments by category (Daftar 5/222, PDF p. 239); liquidity table (Daftar 5/233, PDF p. 250). VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 115 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Pendapatan dari Kontrak dengan Pelanggan","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IFRS 15). Paras. 35, 38, 55, 62–64, 105–109, 116, 118, 120–121, B40, B42, B44–B49 VERIFIED (web, IFRS 15 as adopted, Commission Regulation (EU) 2016/1905, legislation.gov.uk). Paras. 60–61, B39, B50–B51 STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED. IASB/FASB Transition Resource Group, July 2015, Agenda Paper 43 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Commodities","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (point in time vs over time for commodity deliveries). VERIFIED (web, ifrs.org; PwC Viewpoint TRG summary).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 201 (IAS 1), para. 69 (current liabilities) and materiality. Number VERIFIED (web, IAI renumbering list); content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 232 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Instrumen Keuangan: Penyajian","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IAS 32), AG11 (deferred revenue is not a financial liability). VERIFIED (web, NWB reproduction of IAS 32 AL11; HMRC Corporate Finance Manual CFM21130, secondary).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 237 (IAS 37), PSAK 220 (IAS 20), PSAK 107 (IFRS 7), PSAK 55 (IAS 39, superseded by PSAK 109): numbers VERIFIED (web, IAI renumbering list, as in sibling essays); content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Intermediate Accounting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", IFRS edition, chs. 13 and 18. Not on disk; NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb,
       presentation = $p${"deck":"The theory of customer prepayments under PSAK 115 from the five steps to disclosure, worked through PHE's take-or-pay gas balances and set beside the industries that collect before they deliver","genre":null,"author_bio":null,"references":[],"hero_caption":null,"key_takeaways":["Deferred revenue is a contract liability, an obligation to transfer goods or services for which the customer has already paid or for which payment is already due. It is recognised at the earlier of the two and released to revenue as each performance obligation is satisfied.","The amount in the liability depends on the five steps of PSAK 115. Allocation decides how much of a payment belongs to undelivered obligations, a significant financing component can make the liability grow with interest, and customer options and service warranties create obligations that must be deferred even when nothing was prepaid for them.","Rights that customers pay for but never use, such as unused make-up gas under a take-or-pay contract, are breakage. They become revenue in proportion to the rights customers do use or, when the seller does not expect to be entitled to breakage, only once use becomes remote."],"revision_note":null}$p$::jsonb,
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE id = '4af0724c-712f-4fa7-8cff-9e640cf00627' AND updated_at = '2026-10-12T09:47:08.318531+00:00'::timestamptz;
