-- employee-benefits-liabilities-non-current-psak-219
UPDATE public.essays
   SET content = $c$<p><strong>In brief</strong></p><ul><li><p>PSAK 219 sorts employee benefits by when they are paid. Short-term benefits are accrued undiscounted as the work is done; post-employment, other long-term and termination benefits are estimated, discounted, and recognised on their own triggers.</p></li><li><p>A defined contribution plan costs the employer only its contribution. A defined benefit plan leaves the employer holding the actuarial and investment risk, so it carries the present value of the promised benefit, measured by the projected unit credit method, less any plan assets.</p></li><li><p>Service cost and net interest on a defined benefit plan go to profit or loss, while remeasurements (actuarial gains and losses, the return on plan assets beyond interest, and changes in the asset ceiling) go to other comprehensive income and are never recycled. Indonesian statutory severance is an unfunded defined benefit plan of this kind, and since 2022 it is attributed only to the last 24 years of service before retirement age, or from hire if later.</p></li></ul><hr><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report employee benefits liabilities, non-current portion, of 310,011 at 31 December 2024 against 318,604 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12; "Daftar" is the printed page). The line fell 2.7 percent and is 1.0 percent of total assets in both years and 2.0 percent of total liabilities. Two neighbouring lines belong with it, short-term employee benefits of 409,225 (2023: 290,571) in current liabilities and a remeasurement of the employee benefit liability of −610 (2023: 1,430) in other comprehensive income, OCI (Daftar 2/1, PDF p. 14).</p><p>A student should come away able to classify an employee benefit, measure a defined benefit obligation, split its cost between profit or loss and other comprehensive income with journals, and apply this to Indonesian severance.</p><p>All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration. The governing standard is PSAK 219 <em>Imbalan Kerja</em> (formerly PSAK 24, IAS 19 <em>Employee Benefits</em>).</p><h2>1. Four kinds of employee benefit</h2><p>PSAK 219 sorts benefits by when they fall due (para. 8), and the further away the payment, the more estimation it needs.</p><table style="min-width: 100px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Category</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Definition</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>PHE example</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Measurement</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Short-term</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Expected to be settled wholly within 12 months after the year in which the service is rendered</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>salaries, incentives</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>undiscounted, as service is rendered</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Post-employment</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Payable after employment ends</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>pension, severance on retirement, retiree healthcare</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>discounted, actuarial; remeasurements to OCI</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Other long-term</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Everything else that is not short-term, post-employment or termination</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>long service awards (UTD), pre-retirement preparation period (MPPK)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>discounted, actuarial; remeasurements to profit or loss</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Termination</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Paid because the entity ends employment early, or the employee accepts an offer to leave</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>voluntary early retirement packages</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>recognised when the offer can no longer be withdrawn</p></td></tr></tbody></table><h2>2. Short-term benefits</h2><p><strong>Payroll.</strong> Short-term benefits are recognised as the employee works, at the undiscounted amount expected to be paid, as an expense and a liability for whatever is unpaid. Amounts withheld from pay, such as income tax (PPh 21) and the employee share of BPJS contributions, are owed to the tax office and the agencies, not expenses of the employer (see the Other Taxes essay).</p><p><strong>Compensated absences.</strong> <em>Non-accumulating</em> absences, such as sick leave that lapses, are expensed when taken. <em>Accumulating</em> absences carry forward, and the expected cost of the unused entitlement is accrued at the year end, in full if <em>vesting</em> (paid out if unused) and at the expected usage if <em>non-vesting</em> (paras. 13–16). For example (illustration), 400 employees each carry 5 unused days of leave into next year, at a daily pay of 0.25 (USD 250).</p><blockquote><p>Vesting leave = 400 × 5 × 0.25 = 500</p><p>Non-vesting leave, 90% expected to be used = 400 × 5 × 0.25 × 90% = 450</p><p>Dr Employee benefits expense 450 / Cr Accrued compensated absences 450</p></blockquote><p><strong>Bonuses and profit-sharing.</strong> A bonus is recognised when the entity has a present legal or constructive obligation to pay it and can estimate it reliably (para. 19). A <em>constructive obligation</em> arises from established practice, so an entity that pays an incentive every year has no realistic alternative to paying it again. PHE's short-term employee benefits line consists of employee incentives, 409,225 at the end of 2024 (Note 25a, Daftar 5/119, PDF p. 136). Taking its numbers as the starting point, suppose (illustration) the 290,571 accrued for 2023 was settled in 2024 for 285,000, and the 2024 incentive is accrued at 409,225.</p><blockquote><p>Settlement: Dr Short-term employee benefits 290,571 / Cr Cash 285,000 / Cr Employee benefits expense 5,571</p><p>Accrual: Dr Employee benefits expense 409,225 / Cr Short-term employee benefits 409,225</p><p>Net 2024 incentive expense = 409,225 − 5,571 = 403,654</p></blockquote><p>The over-accrual, a change in estimate, reduces this year's expense. The year-end balance equals 28.2 percent of PHE's 2024 personnel costs of 1,453,337 (688,343 + 510,007 + 254,987, Notes 37–39, Daftar 5/149, PDF p. 166).</p><h2>3. Post-employment benefits: defined contribution and defined benefit</h2><p>Who carries the risk decides the accounting. In a <em>defined contribution</em> (DC) plan the employer pays fixed contributions into a fund and has no obligation to pay more if the fund falls short, so the employee bears the investment risk (para. 28). In a <em>defined benefit</em> (DB) plan the employer promises the benefit itself, so if the fund earns less or employees live longer the employer pays the difference (para. 30).</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Defined contribution</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Defined benefit</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Employer promises</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>a contribution</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>a benefit</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Risk borne by</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>employee</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>employer</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Expense</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>the contribution for the period</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>service cost + net interest</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Balance sheet</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>unpaid contributions only</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>present value of obligation − plan assets</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>For example (illustration), a subsidiary pays 5 percent of a monthly payroll of 2,000 into a financial institution pension fund (<em>Dana Pensiun Lembaga Keuangan</em>, DPLK) as a DC plan, and has not yet paid December's contribution at the year end.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Employee benefits expense 100 / Cr Accrued pension contributions 100</p></blockquote><p>PHE's policy expenses DC contributions as incurred (Note 3q(ii), Daftar 5/64, PDF p. 81). The rest of this page is about DB plans.</p><h2>4. Measuring the obligation: the projected unit credit method</h2><p>PSAK 219 requires the <em>projected unit credit method</em> (PUCM), which measures a promise earned a little each year in three steps (para. 67).</p><ol><li><p>Project the benefit payable at retirement using the expected final salary.</p></li><li><p>Attribute the benefit to years of service using the plan's formula (paras. 70–71), so each year of service earns one "unit".</p></li><li><p>Discount the units earned to date at a rate set by reference to high-quality corporate bond yields, or government bond yields where no deep corporate bond market exists, in the currency and term of the obligation (para. 83). PHE uses government bond rates for that reason (Note 3q(iii), PDF p. 81).</p></li></ol><p>The present value of units earned to date is the <em>defined benefit obligation</em> (DBO), of this year's unit the <em>current service cost</em>, and the unwinding of discount on the opening DBO the <em>interest cost</em>.</p><p>For example (illustration), take PHE's 2024 salary increase of 7 percent and a discount rate of 7.1 percent, the top of its 4.7–7.1 percent range, as the starting point (Note 25b, Daftar 5/121, PDF p. 138). An employee has 20 years of service, retires in 5 years with 25 years, and the plan pays a lump sum of one month's final salary per year of service. Current monthly salary is 2.0 (USD 2,000). Mortality and resignation are ignored.</p><blockquote><p>Final monthly salary = 2.0 × 1.07^5 = 2.80510</p><p>Benefit at retirement = 25 × 2.80510 = 70.128; each year of service earns 2.80510</p><p>Opening DBO = 20 × 2.80510 / 1.071^5 = 39.814</p></blockquote><table style="min-width: 125px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Year</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Opening DBO</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Interest cost (7.1%)</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Current service cost</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Closing DBO</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>39.814</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2.827</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2.132</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>44.772</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>44.772</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3.179</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2.283</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>50.235</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>50.235</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3.567</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2.446</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>56.247</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>4</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>56.247</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3.994</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2.619</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>62.859</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>5</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>62.859</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>4.463</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2.805</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>70.128</p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>Current service cost, year 1 = 2.80510 / 1.071^4 = 2.132</p><p>Closing DBO, year 1 = 21 × 2.80510 / 1.071^4 = 44.772 = 39.814 + 2.827 + 2.132</p></blockquote><p>Service cost rises each year although the unit is the same, because it is discounted over a shorter period. The DBO uses the <em>final</em> salary, which is what "projected" means. An actuary does the same for thousands of employees with probabilities of death, disability and resignation at each age (PHE cites Indonesian Mortality Table IV).</p><h2>5. Plan assets, the net liability and the three components of cost</h2><p><em>Plan assets</em> are assets held by a separate fund, or qualifying insurance policies, available only to pay employee benefits and not to the employer's creditors (para. 8). They are measured at fair value and netted against the DBO.</p><blockquote><p>Net defined benefit liability = DBO − fair value of plan assets (adjusted for the asset ceiling)</p></blockquote><p>The cost for the period has three components, and the standard sends each to a different place (para. 120).</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Component</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Contents</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Where</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Service cost</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>current service cost, past service cost, settlement gains and losses</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>profit or loss</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Net interest</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>discount rate × net liability (the opening balance when cash flows fall at the year end, as here)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>profit or loss</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Remeasurements</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>actuarial gains and losses on the DBO; return on plan assets excluding interest income; change in the asset ceiling</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>other comprehensive income, never reclassified (para. 122)</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Net interest applies the same discount rate to the DBO and the plan assets (para. 123), so a company cannot report a high expected return on risky assets as profit; whatever the fund earns above or below that rate is a remeasurement.</p><p>Kieso tracks this with a <em>pension worksheet</em>, with journal columns on the left and memo columns for the DBO and plan assets on the right. For example, take PHE's 2024 opening DBO of 635,792 and plan assets of 329,152 as the starting point (Note 25b, Daftar 5/122, PDF p. 139). Assume for illustration a single discount rate of 7.1 percent, current service cost of 19,285 (PHE's reported figure), employer contributions of 74,643, benefits of 30,400 paid out of the fund (PHE's DBO movement; its asset movement shows 31,410), all cash flows at the year end, no exchange differences, and closing measurements by the actuary of 657,210 and 387,381.</p><blockquote><p>Interest cost = 635,792 × 7.1% = 45,141.2; interest income = 329,152 × 7.1% = 23,369.8</p><p>Net interest = (635,792 − 329,152) × 7.1% = 306,640 × 7.1% = 21,771.4</p><p>Expected DBO = 635,792 + 19,285 + 45,141.2 − 30,400 = 669,818.2; actual 657,210; actuarial gain = 12,608.2</p><p>Expected plan assets = 329,152 + 23,369.8 + 74,643 − 30,400 = 396,764.8; actual 387,381; return below interest = 9,383.8</p><p>Net remeasurement = 12,608.2 − 9,383.8 = 3,224.4 gain</p></blockquote><table style="min-width: 175px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Item</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Expense (P&amp;L)</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>OCI</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cash</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Net liability</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>DBO (memo)</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Plan assets (memo)</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Opening</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>306,640.0 Cr</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>635,792.0 Cr</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>329,152.0 Dr</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Service cost</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>19,285.0 Dr</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>19,285.0 Cr</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Interest cost / income</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>21,771.4 Dr</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>45,141.2 Cr</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>23,369.8 Dr</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Contributions</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>74,643.0 Cr</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>74,643.0 Dr</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Benefits paid by fund</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>30,400.0 Dr</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>30,400.0 Cr</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Remeasurements</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,224.4 Cr</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>12,608.2 Dr</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>9,383.8 Cr</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Journal</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>41,056.4 Dr</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,224.4 Cr</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>74,643.0 Cr</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>36,811.0 Dr</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Closing</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>269,829.0 Cr</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>657,210.0 Cr</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>387,381.0 Dr</p></td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>Check: 306,640 + 41,056.4 − 74,643 − 3,224.4 = 269,829.0 = 657,210 − 387,381</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>Dr Employee benefits expense 41,056.4 / Cr Net defined benefit liability 41,056.4</p><p>Dr Net defined benefit liability 74,643.0 / Cr Cash 74,643.0</p><p>Dr Net defined benefit liability 3,224.4 / Cr OCI, remeasurement of employee benefits 3,224.4</p></blockquote><p>Benefits paid by the fund need no employer entry. The closing 269,829 matches PHE's reported deficit before the asset ceiling, though PHE's own path differs (section 10) and its DBO appears to include some other long-term benefits. Deferred tax on the remeasurement goes to OCI with it (see the Deferred Tax essay).</p><p><strong>The asset ceiling.</strong> When a plan is in surplus, the employer may recognise the surplus only up to the present value of the economic benefits available to it as refunds or reductions in future contributions (para. 64). For example (illustration), a plan with a surplus of 10,000 from which the employer can recover only 8,348 through lower future contributions shows an asset of 8,348, with a ceiling effect of 10,000 − 8,348 = 1,652, PHE's 2024 ceiling effect. The ceiling is applied plan by plan, so a group can show a ceiling effect inside an overall net liability.</p><h2>6. Past service cost, curtailments and settlements</h2><p>Plan changes hit profit or loss at once. <em>Past service cost</em> is the change in the DBO for service already rendered, caused by a plan amendment (introducing or changing a plan) or a <em>curtailment</em> (a significant reduction in the number of employees covered). It is recognised as an expense at the earlier of when the amendment or curtailment occurs and when related restructuring costs or termination benefits are recognised (para. 103). For example (illustration), a subsidiary raises a lump-sum benefit by 10 percent for a group whose DBO is 50,000.</p><blockquote><p>Past service cost = 50,000 × 10% = 5,000</p><p>Dr Employee benefits expense 5,000 / Cr Net defined benefit liability 5,000</p></blockquote><p>Since the 2011 revision of IAS 19 (in Indonesia from 2015) past service cost is no longer spread over the vesting period.</p><p>A <em>settlement</em> removes the obligation altogether, for example when the fund buys annuities from an insurer that takes over the payments. The gain or loss is the difference between the DBO settled and the price paid. For example (illustration), the fund pays an insurer 41,500 to take over pensions in payment with a DBO of 40,000.</p><blockquote><p>Settlement loss = 41,500 − 40,000 = 1,500</p><p>Memo: DBO −40,000; plan assets −41,500</p><p>Dr Employee benefits expense 1,500 / Cr Net defined benefit liability 1,500</p></blockquote><p>A purchased policy settles the plan only if the insurer, not the employer, becomes obliged to the pensioners.</p><h2>7. Indonesian severance as a defined benefit plan</h2><p>Indonesian labour law promises every permanent employee a lump sum at retirement set by a formula, so the employer carries a DB plan under PSAK 219, unfunded unless it sets assets aside. The formula sits in UU 13/2003 as amended by the Job Creation law (UU 11/2020, replaced by Perpu 2/2022 and enacted as UU 6/2023), and in its implementing regulation PP 35/2021. On retirement an employee receives (PP 35/2021, art. 56):</p><ul><li><p>severance pay (<em>uang pesangon</em>) of 1.75 times the art. 40(2) scale, which rises from 1 month's wages for under one year of service to a maximum of 9 months from 8 years;</p></li><li><p>a long-service payment (<em>uang penghargaan masa kerja</em>, UPMK) of 1 times the art. 40(3) scale, from 2 months at 3 years to a maximum of 10 months from 24 years;</p></li><li><p>compensation for rights (<em>uang penggantian hak</em>), such as unused leave.</p></li></ul><blockquote><p>Maximum on retirement = 1.75 × 9 + 10 = 25.75 months' wages, reached at 24 years of service</p></blockquote><p>Employers pay the higher of the statutory amount and their own plan or collective labour agreement, as PHE's policy says (Note 3q, Daftar 5/63, PDF p. 80). Since Constitutional Court decision 168/PUU-XXI/2023 (31 October 2024) the statutory scale is a minimum (<em>paling sedikit</em>), and the court ordered a new labour law, so these multiples may change.</p><p><strong>When the benefit is earned.</strong> For years many Indonesian actuaries attributed this benefit straight-line from the date of hire. In an agenda decision published in May 2021 the IFRS Interpretations Committee considered a plan that pays only to those employed at retirement age, capped at a number of years of consecutive service immediately before retirement, and concluded that the benefit is attributed only to the capped years before retirement age, since service before then does not reduce the service still needed. In April 2022 the DSAK IAI, the Indonesian standard setter, found that the statutory scheme has the same fact pattern. With retirement at 56 and the UPMK capped at 24 years, the obligation first arises at age 32, and the benefit is attributed to service between 32 and 56. Entities that had attributed from hire were told to assess the effect under the PSAK 25 (now PSAK 208) requirements for a change in accounting policy.</p><p>For example (illustration), an employee joined at 25, is now 40 with 15 years of service, and retires at 56 with 31. Current monthly wage is 1.5 (USD 1,500), with PHE's 7 percent salary increase and 7.1 percent discount rate, and no resignations or deaths.</p><blockquote><p>Final monthly wage = 1.5 × 1.07^16 = 4.4282; benefit = 25.75 × 4.4282 = 114.027</p><p>Present value of the full benefit = 114.027 / 1.071^16 = 38.052</p></blockquote><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Attributed from hire (age 25–56)</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Attributed from age 32 (2022 clarification)</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Attribution period</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 years</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>24 years</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Years earned at age 40</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>15</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>8</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>DBO</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>38.052 × 15 / 31 = 18.412</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>38.052 × 8 / 24 = 12.684</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Benefit earned per year</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>114.027 / 31 = 3.678</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>114.027 / 24 = 4.751</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The later start cuts this employee's DBO by 5.728, or 31.1 percent, and raises the annual unit by 29.2 percent (31/24), so the cost moves later in the career. An employee hired at 35 would see no change, because attribution starts at hire. The younger the hiring age across a workforce, the larger the fall in the opening liability.</p><p><strong>Funding it.</strong> PP 35/2021 art. 58 lets employer contributions to a pension programme count towards statutory severance, with the employer paying any shortfall, so a fund held for that purpose is a plan asset of the severance DB plan and does not turn it into a DC plan. PHE funds its service award (PAP, <em>Penghargaan Atas Pengabdian</em>) through such a DPLK programme (PPUKP) and treats its assets as plan assets (Note 25b, Daftar 5/120, PDF p. 137). All its other benefits are unfunded.</p><h2>8. Other long-term and termination benefits</h2><p><strong>Other long-term benefits</strong> are measured with the same PUCM, but every element, remeasurements included, goes to profit or loss, because they are less uncertain than post-employment benefits (paras. 154–156). PHE's are long-service awards (<em>Ulang Tahun Dinas</em>, UTD), in cash or goods, and the pre-retirement preparation period (MPPK) (Note 3q(iv), Daftar 5/65, PDF p. 82). Its gold price and gold inflation assumptions suggest some awards are paid in gold.</p><p>For example (illustration, in Rp million), an award of 10 grams of gold at 25 years of service, earned evenly. An employee has 22 years of service. Take PHE's 2024 gold price of Rp1,462,500 per gram, gold inflation of 8 percent and the 7.1 percent discount rate.</p><blockquote><p>Award value in 3 years = 10 × 1.4625 × 1.08^3 = Rp18.423 million</p><p>Obligation = 18.423 × 22 / 25 / 1.071^3 = Rp13.197 million</p></blockquote><p>A 10 percent rise in the gold price adds about Rp1.320 million to the obligation, charged straight to profit or loss.</p><p><strong>Termination benefits</strong> arise from the decision to end employment, not from service (para. 159). Only the extra paid to induce early leaving is a termination benefit. It is recognised at the earlier of when the entity can no longer withdraw the offer and when it recognises related restructuring costs under PSAK 237 (para. 165). For example (illustration), 120 employees accept a voluntary early-retirement package of 30 each, and each already carries a recognised DBO of 20.</p><blockquote><p>Package = 120 × 30 = 3,600; DBO settled = 120 × 20 = 2,400; termination benefit = 3,600 − 2,400 = 1,200</p><p>Dr Net defined benefit liability 2,400, Dr Termination benefits expense 1,200 / Cr Cash 3,600</p></blockquote><h2>9. Employee benefits across industries</h2><p><strong>State-owned groups and banks with their own pension funds.</strong> Many run employer pension funds (<em>Dana Pensiun Pemberi Kerja</em>, DPPK) offering final-salary DB pensions alongside statutory severance. With large plan assets, a fall in bond yields or in asset returns can swing equity through OCI. Closing a DB plan to future accrual and moving staff to DC is a plan amendment, and any fall in the DBO, for example from removing the final-salary link, is a negative past service cost in profit or loss.</p><p><strong>Labour-intensive industries.</strong> In plantations, textiles, footwear and manufacturing the unfunded severance obligation can be large relative to equity, and the 2022 attribution change mattered most where staff are hired young. In-kind benefits such as plantation workers' rice and housing (<em>natura</em>) are short-term benefits at cost.</p><p><strong>Pension funds, insurers and share-based pay.</strong> A DPPK reports its own statements under PSAK 226 (formerly PSAK 18, IAS 26), an insurer carries annuities bought to settle a plan under PSAK 117 (formerly PSAK 74, IFRS 17), and share-based pay follows PSAK 102 (IFRS 2).</p><p><strong>Upstream oil and gas.</strong> An operator employs a contract area's staff on behalf of all partners, who carry their shares of benefit costs through joint billings, and under a cost recovery production sharing contract (PSC) those costs are generally recoverable. PHE notes that such operators provide post-employment benefits to their own permanent staff (Note 3q, PDF p. 81).</p><h2>10. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Note 25</h2><p>PHE gives most of the disclosures PSAK 219 asks for: plan descriptions, reconciliations, the asset mix, assumptions, sensitivities and the timing of payments (Note 25b, Daftar 5/119–5/124, PDF pp. 136–141).</p><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p></p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2023</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Post-employment, direct hire</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>221,421</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>237,309</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Post-employment, Pertamina secondees</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>46,189</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>50,942</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Other long-term, direct hire</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>38,530</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>9,407</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Other long-term, secondees</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,871</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>20,946</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Employee benefits liabilities, non-current</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>310,011</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>318,604</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The funded status reconciles to the line only after one step.</p><blockquote><p>DBO 657,210 − plan assets 387,381 = deficit 269,829; + asset ceiling 1,652 = 271,481</p><p>271,481 + other long-term, direct hire 38,530 = 310,011 (2023: 309,197 + 9,407 = 318,604)</p></blockquote><p>The 271,481 is 3,871 more than the post-employment total of 267,610, exactly the secondees' other long-term benefits (2023: 20,946). The funded-status table and the DBO movement therefore appear to include them, and the post-employment liability movement (267,610) does not. PHE does not say so.</p><blockquote><p>Funded ratio = 387,381 / 657,210 = 58.9% (2023: 329,152 / 635,792 = 51.8%)</p><p>Plan assets: money market 80%, debt instruments 8%, equity 6%, investment funds 6% (2023: 57%, 29%, 8%, 6%)</p></blockquote><p>Only PAP is funded, so the ratio mixes one funded plan with unfunded ones. It rose on contributions of 74,643 against benefits of 31,410, and the fund moved into money market instruments. Undiscounted expected payments total 1,777,541, 2.7 times the DBO.</p><p>The sensitivity table gives a DBO of 526,700 at a discount rate 1 point higher and 637,468 at 1 point lower. An approximate <em>duration</em>, the percentage change in the DBO per point of rate, is (637,468 − 526,700) / (2 × 0.01 × 582,084) = 9.5 years, where 582,084 is the midpoint. On the reported DBO it would be 8.4 years. Both figures lie below that DBO, as in 2023, so they were probably run on a narrower population or an earlier date. PHE does not disclose the weighted average duration that para. 147(c) requires.</p><p>The OCI remeasurement appears as four different figures across the statements and Note 25, and interest income, the return on plan assets, contributions, benefits and the ceiling change also differ between tables (see Least-confident). PHE's policy still describes the pre-2015 rules for past service cost and curtailment (Note 3q(iii), PDF pp. 81–82).</p><p>Deferred tax on these liabilities totals 8,711 + 27,119 = 35,830, with 161 charged to OCI in 2024 (Note 43e, Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172).</p><h2>11. Upstream note: secondees from Pertamina</h2><p>Part of PHE's workforce are Pertamina employees seconded to the group. Since 2020 Pertamina's policy has required each subsidiary to recognise its share of six benefits Pertamina owes those secondees (the Pertamina pension fund DPP, PAP, retiree healthcare, repatriation, MPPK and UTD) for the period they serve there (Note 25, Daftar 5/118, PDF p. 135). Before 2020 subsidiaries were charged only an allocated expense. A DB plan that shares risk between entities under common control is not a multi-employer plan, and each entity recognises the cost charged to it under the group's agreement or policy (paras. 40–41). PHE carries the secondees' share in its DBO (46,189 post-employment at the end of 2024), and its expense table shows a separate "PT Pertamina (Persero) allocation" of 5,108 (2023: 11,658), presumably the service cost Pertamina charges under Note 3q; the FS do not say.</p><h2>12. Related lines</h2><ul><li><p><code>short-term-employee-benefits</code>: the incentives payable in section 2.</p></li><li><p><code>oci-remeasurement-employee-benefits</code>: the remeasurements in section 5.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/production-expenses">production-expenses</a>, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/production-expenses">general-administrative-expenses</a>, <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/production-expenses">expense-from-other-operations</a>: where service cost and net interest are charged.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets">deferred-tax-assets</a> and <a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets">deferred-tax-liabilities</a>: the temporary differences on these liabilities.</p></li><li><p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" class="text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80" href="/accounting/fsli/other-taxes-receivable-current">other-taxes-payable</a>: withholding tax on salaries.</p></li></ul><h3>Least-confident assertions</h3><ul><li><p>That PHE's funded-status table and DBO movement include secondees' other long-term benefits is inferred from the 3,871 and 20,946 differences matching to the unit in both years. Direct-hire and secondee long-term balances also swapped size between years (9,407 → 38,530; 20,946 → 3,871), which may mean relabelling, so the label is less certain than the arithmetic. The English text of Note 25b dates the actuarial calculation to 2023 and the Indonesian to 2024; this page follows the Indonesian.</p></li><li><p>PHE's notes disagree in detail. The OCI remeasurement is −610 in the statement (labelled a gain, possibly net of the 161 of deferred tax), 13,584 in Note 25's OCI table (labelled gains although the salary assumption rose), +11,397 in the DBO movement and +9,566 in the net liability movement. Interest income on plan assets is 22,333 and 20,760; the return beyond interest appears as −535, −241 (in profit or loss, against para. 127 and PHE's policy) and 1,903; contributions are 74,643 and 74,360; benefits reduce the DBO by 30,400 and plan assets by 31,410, although only PAP is funded; the asset ceiling fell by 905 but the OCI table shows 304. The 2023 other long-term movement puts 2,269 in OCI against policy. Note 4 gives the carrying amount as US$30,271 (2023: US$125,528) in Indonesian and US$125,528 for both years in English (Daftar 5/85, PDF p. 102). MPPK is other long-term in Note 3q(iv) but listed under post-employment in Note 25b.</p></li><li><p>The sensitivity figures sit below the reported DBO at both rates in both years, so the 9.5-year (8.4 on the reported DBO) duration is indicative only. That the 5,108 allocation is Pertamina's service-cost charge is a reading; the FS do not say.</p></li><li><p>That some UTD awards are paid in gold is read from the gold assumptions; the FS do not say. The industry paragraphs (DPPK closures, plantation natura, the size of the 2022 effect on labour-intensive firms) are general practice, not researched for named companies. The treatment of PSC cost recovery of benefit costs is stated generally; PHE's FS do not describe it for employee benefits.</p></li><li><p>The severance example ignores resignation, death and disability, which an actuary would weight in, although the statutory formula also sets those benefits, and ignores <em>uang penggantian hak</em>. After decision 168/PUU-XXI/2023 a new labour law was ordered; its status at the date of this page was not checked, and it may change the multiples.</p></li></ul><h2>References</h2><ol><li><p>PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12); statement of profit or loss and OCI (Daftar 2/1, PDF p. 14); Note 3q (Daftar 5/63–5/65, PDF pp. 80–82); Note 4 (Daftar 5/85, PDF p. 102); Note 25 (Daftar 5/118–5/124, PDF pp. 135–141); Notes 37–39 (Daftar 5/149, PDF p. 166); Note 43e (Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172). Indonesian and English columns both read. VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk).</p></li><li><p>The page follows the order of Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, <em>Intermediate Accounting</em>, IFRS edition, chs. 13 and 20, and the structure of PSAK 219. The book was not opened for this page. TEXTBOOK — NOT OPENED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 219 <em>Imbalan Kerja</em> (IAS 19 <em>Employee Benefits</em>, 2011), paras. 8, 13–16, 19, 40–41, 64, 67, 70–73, 83, 103–105, 120, 122–123, 127, 147(c), 153–156, 159–160, 165. VERIFIED (web, IAS 19 as adopted by Commission Regulation (EU) No 475/2012, eur-lex.europa.eu; paras. 104–105, 127, 147(c) confirmed in the independent recheck). Paras. 28 and 30 (DC and DB definitions), 135–147 (disclosure) and the 2015 Indonesian effective date of the 2011 revision: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>DSAK IAI, <em>Siaran Pers – Pengatribusian Imbalan pada Periode Jasa</em>, April 2022, citing IFRS Interpretations Committee agenda decision "Attributing Benefit to Periods of Service (IAS 19)", May 2021. VERIFIED (web, iaiglobal.or.id PDF, read in full).</p></li><li><p>PP 35/2021, arts. 40(2)–(4), 56 and 58. VERIFIED (web, secondary: BP Lawyers; detik.com quoting art. 56; hukumonline.com and Bisnis.com on art. 58). Constitutional Court decision 168/PUU-XXI/2023 (31 October 2024), art. 156(2) read as <em>paling sedikit</em>: VERIFIED (web, secondary: hukumonline.com, "Putusan MK Ini Buka Peluang Buruh Terima Pesangon Lebih Tinggi"); the court's text not opened. UU 6/2023 enacting Perpu 2/2022 and revoking UU 11/2020: VERIFIED (web, peraturan.bpk.go.id).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 226 (IAS 26, formerly PSAK 18): title VERIFIED (web, secondary: Binus Accounting renumbering table). PSAK 102 (IFRS 2), PSAK 117 (IFRS 17, formerly PSAK 74), PSAK 208 (IAS 8, formerly PSAK 25), PSAK 212 (IAS 12), PSAK 237 (IAS 37): numbers per IAI renumbering rule; content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li></ol>$c$,
       content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In brief","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 219 sorts employee benefits by when they are paid. Short-term benefits are accrued undiscounted as the work is done; post-employment, other long-term and termination benefits are estimated, discounted, and recognised on their own triggers.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A defined contribution plan costs the employer only its contribution. A defined benefit plan leaves the employer holding the actuarial and investment risk, so it carries the present value of the promised benefit, measured by the projected unit credit method, less any plan assets.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Service cost and net interest on a defined benefit plan go to profit or loss, while remeasurements (actuarial gains and losses, the return on plan assets beyond interest, and changes in the asset ceiling) go to other comprehensive income and are never recycled. Indonesian statutory severance is an unfunded defined benefit plan of this kind, and since 2022 it is attributed only to the last 24 years of service before retirement age, or from hire if later.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi and subsidiaries (PHE) report employee benefits liabilities, non-current portion, of 310,011 at 31 December 2024 against 318,604 a year earlier, in USD thousand (PHE FS 2024, statement of financial position, Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12; \"Daftar\" is the printed page). The line fell 2.7 percent and is 1.0 percent of total assets in both years and 2.0 percent of total liabilities. Two neighbouring lines belong with it, short-term employee benefits of 409,225 (2023: 290,571) in current liabilities and a remeasurement of the employee benefit liability of −610 (2023: 1,430) in other comprehensive income, OCI (Daftar 2/1, PDF p. 14).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A student should come away able to classify an employee benefit, measure a defined benefit obligation, split its cost between profit or loss and other comprehensive income with journals, and apply this to Indonesian severance.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"All amounts are in USD thousand unless stated. Figures from PHE's statements are cited to their note. Every other number is labelled an illustration. The governing standard is PSAK 219 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Imbalan Kerja","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 24, IAS 19 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Employee Benefits","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":").","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1. Four kinds of employee benefit","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 219 sorts benefits by when they fall due (para. 8), and the further away the payment, the more estimation it needs.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Category","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Definition","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE example","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Measurement","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Short-term","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Expected to be settled wholly within 12 months after the year in which the service is rendered","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"salaries, incentives","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"undiscounted, as service is rendered","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Post-employment","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Payable after employment ends","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"pension, severance on retirement, retiree healthcare","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"discounted, actuarial; remeasurements to OCI","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Other long-term","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Everything else that is not short-term, post-employment or termination","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"long service awards (UTD), pre-retirement preparation period (MPPK)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"discounted, actuarial; remeasurements to profit or loss","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Termination","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Paid because the entity ends employment early, or the employee accepts an offer to leave","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"voluntary early retirement packages","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"recognised when the offer can no longer be withdrawn","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2. Short-term benefits","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Payroll.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Short-term benefits are recognised as the employee works, at the undiscounted amount expected to be paid, as an expense and a liability for whatever is unpaid. Amounts withheld from pay, such as income tax (PPh 21) and the employee share of BPJS contributions, are owed to the tax office and the agencies, not expenses of the employer (see the Other Taxes essay).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Compensated absences.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"Non-accumulating","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" absences, such as sick leave that lapses, are expensed when taken. ","type":"text"},{"text":"Accumulating","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" absences carry forward, and the expected cost of the unused entitlement is accrued at the year end, in full if ","type":"text"},{"text":"vesting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (paid out if unused) and at the expected usage if ","type":"text"},{"text":"non-vesting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (paras. 13–16). For example (illustration), 400 employees each carry 5 unused days of leave into next year, at a daily pay of 0.25 (USD 250).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Vesting leave = 400 × 5 × 0.25 = 500","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Non-vesting leave, 90% expected to be used = 400 × 5 × 0.25 × 90% = 450","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Employee benefits expense 450 / Cr Accrued compensated absences 450","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Bonuses and profit-sharing.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A bonus is recognised when the entity has a present legal or constructive obligation to pay it and can estimate it reliably (para. 19). A ","type":"text"},{"text":"constructive obligation","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" arises from established practice, so an entity that pays an incentive every year has no realistic alternative to paying it again. PHE's short-term employee benefits line consists of employee incentives, 409,225 at the end of 2024 (Note 25a, Daftar 5/119, PDF p. 136). Taking its numbers as the starting point, suppose (illustration) the 290,571 accrued for 2023 was settled in 2024 for 285,000, and the 2024 incentive is accrued at 409,225.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Settlement: Dr Short-term employee benefits 290,571 / Cr Cash 285,000 / Cr Employee benefits expense 5,571","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Accrual: Dr Employee benefits expense 409,225 / Cr Short-term employee benefits 409,225","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net 2024 incentive expense = 409,225 − 5,571 = 403,654","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The over-accrual, a change in estimate, reduces this year's expense. The year-end balance equals 28.2 percent of PHE's 2024 personnel costs of 1,453,337 (688,343 + 510,007 + 254,987, Notes 37–39, Daftar 5/149, PDF p. 166).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3. Post-employment benefits: defined contribution and defined benefit","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Who carries the risk decides the accounting. In a ","type":"text"},{"text":"defined contribution","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (DC) plan the employer pays fixed contributions into a fund and has no obligation to pay more if the fund falls short, so the employee bears the investment risk (para. 28). In a ","type":"text"},{"text":"defined benefit","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (DB) plan the employer promises the benefit itself, so if the fund earns less or employees live longer the employer pays the difference (para. 30).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Defined contribution","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Defined benefit","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Employer promises","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"a contribution","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"a benefit","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Risk borne by","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"employee","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"employer","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Expense","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"the contribution for the period","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"service cost + net interest","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Balance sheet","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"unpaid contributions only","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"present value of obligation − plan assets","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), a subsidiary pays 5 percent of a monthly payroll of 2,000 into a financial institution pension fund (","type":"text"},{"text":"Dana Pensiun Lembaga Keuangan","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", DPLK) as a DC plan, and has not yet paid December's contribution at the year end.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Employee benefits expense 100 / Cr Accrued pension contributions 100","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's policy expenses DC contributions as incurred (Note 3q(ii), Daftar 5/64, PDF p. 81). The rest of this page is about DB plans.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4. Measuring the obligation: the projected unit credit method","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 219 requires the ","type":"text"},{"text":"projected unit credit method","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (PUCM), which measures a promise earned a little each year in three steps (para. 67).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Project the benefit payable at retirement using the expected final salary.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Attribute the benefit to years of service using the plan's formula (paras. 70–71), so each year of service earns one \"unit\".","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Discount the units earned to date at a rate set by reference to high-quality corporate bond yields, or government bond yields where no deep corporate bond market exists, in the currency and term of the obligation (para. 83). PHE uses government bond rates for that reason (Note 3q(iii), PDF p. 81).","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The present value of units earned to date is the ","type":"text"},{"text":"defined benefit obligation","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (DBO), of this year's unit the ","type":"text"},{"text":"current service cost","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", and the unwinding of discount on the opening DBO the ","type":"text"},{"text":"interest cost","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), take PHE's 2024 salary increase of 7 percent and a discount rate of 7.1 percent, the top of its 4.7–7.1 percent range, as the starting point (Note 25b, Daftar 5/121, PDF p. 138). An employee has 20 years of service, retires in 5 years with 25 years, and the plan pays a lump sum of one month's final salary per year of service. Current monthly salary is 2.0 (USD 2,000). Mortality and resignation are ignored.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Final monthly salary = 2.0 × 1.07^5 = 2.80510","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Benefit at retirement = 25 × 2.80510 = 70.128; each year of service earns 2.80510","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Opening DBO = 20 × 2.80510 / 1.071^5 = 39.814","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Year","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Opening DBO","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest cost (7.1%)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Current service cost","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Closing DBO","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"39.814","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2.827","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2.132","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"44.772","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"44.772","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3.179","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2.283","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"50.235","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"50.235","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3.567","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2.446","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"56.247","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"56.247","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3.994","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2.619","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"62.859","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"62.859","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4.463","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2.805","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"70.128","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Current service cost, year 1 = 2.80510 / 1.071^4 = 2.132","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Closing DBO, year 1 = 21 × 2.80510 / 1.071^4 = 44.772 = 39.814 + 2.827 + 2.132","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Service cost rises each year although the unit is the same, because it is discounted over a shorter period. The DBO uses the ","type":"text"},{"text":"final","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" salary, which is what \"projected\" means. An actuary does the same for thousands of employees with probabilities of death, disability and resignation at each age (PHE cites Indonesian Mortality Table IV).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"5. Plan assets, the net liability and the three components of cost","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Plan assets","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" are assets held by a separate fund, or qualifying insurance policies, available only to pay employee benefits and not to the employer's creditors (para. 8). They are measured at fair value and netted against the DBO.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net defined benefit liability = DBO − fair value of plan assets (adjusted for the asset ceiling)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The cost for the period has three components, and the standard sends each to a different place (para. 120).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Component","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Contents","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Where","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Service cost","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"current service cost, past service cost, settlement gains and losses","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"profit or loss","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net interest","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"discount rate × net liability (the opening balance when cash flows fall at the year end, as here)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"profit or loss","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Remeasurements","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"actuarial gains and losses on the DBO; return on plan assets excluding interest income; change in the asset ceiling","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"other comprehensive income, never reclassified (para. 122)","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net interest applies the same discount rate to the DBO and the plan assets (para. 123), so a company cannot report a high expected return on risky assets as profit; whatever the fund earns above or below that rate is a remeasurement.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kieso tracks this with a ","type":"text"},{"text":"pension worksheet","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", with journal columns on the left and memo columns for the DBO and plan assets on the right. For example, take PHE's 2024 opening DBO of 635,792 and plan assets of 329,152 as the starting point (Note 25b, Daftar 5/122, PDF p. 139). Assume for illustration a single discount rate of 7.1 percent, current service cost of 19,285 (PHE's reported figure), employer contributions of 74,643, benefits of 30,400 paid out of the fund (PHE's DBO movement; its asset movement shows 31,410), all cash flows at the year end, no exchange differences, and closing measurements by the actuary of 657,210 and 387,381.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest cost = 635,792 × 7.1% = 45,141.2; interest income = 329,152 × 7.1% = 23,369.8","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net interest = (635,792 − 329,152) × 7.1% = 306,640 × 7.1% = 21,771.4","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Expected DBO = 635,792 + 19,285 + 45,141.2 − 30,400 = 669,818.2; actual 657,210; actuarial gain = 12,608.2","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Expected plan assets = 329,152 + 23,369.8 + 74,643 − 30,400 = 396,764.8; actual 387,381; return below interest = 9,383.8","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net remeasurement = 12,608.2 − 9,383.8 = 3,224.4 gain","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Item","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Expense (P&L)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"OCI","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net liability","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"DBO (memo)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Plan assets (memo)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Opening","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"306,640.0 Cr","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"635,792.0 Cr","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"329,152.0 Dr","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Service cost","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"19,285.0 Dr","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"19,285.0 Cr","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest cost / income","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"21,771.4 Dr","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"45,141.2 Cr","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"23,369.8 Dr","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Contributions","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"74,643.0 Cr","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"74,643.0 Dr","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Benefits paid by fund","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"30,400.0 Dr","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"30,400.0 Cr","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Remeasurements","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,224.4 Cr","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"12,608.2 Dr","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9,383.8 Cr","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Journal","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"41,056.4 Dr","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,224.4 Cr","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"74,643.0 Cr","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"36,811.0 Dr","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Closing","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"269,829.0 Cr","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"657,210.0 Cr","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"387,381.0 Dr","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Check: 306,640 + 41,056.4 − 74,643 − 3,224.4 = 269,829.0 = 657,210 − 387,381","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Employee benefits expense 41,056.4 / Cr Net defined benefit liability 41,056.4","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Net defined benefit liability 74,643.0 / Cr Cash 74,643.0","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Net defined benefit liability 3,224.4 / Cr OCI, remeasurement of employee benefits 3,224.4","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Benefits paid by the fund need no employer entry. The closing 269,829 matches PHE's reported deficit before the asset ceiling, though PHE's own path differs (section 10) and its DBO appears to include some other long-term benefits. Deferred tax on the remeasurement goes to OCI with it (see the Deferred Tax essay).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The asset ceiling.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" When a plan is in surplus, the employer may recognise the surplus only up to the present value of the economic benefits available to it as refunds or reductions in future contributions (para. 64). For example (illustration), a plan with a surplus of 10,000 from which the employer can recover only 8,348 through lower future contributions shows an asset of 8,348, with a ceiling effect of 10,000 − 8,348 = 1,652, PHE's 2024 ceiling effect. The ceiling is applied plan by plan, so a group can show a ceiling effect inside an overall net liability.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"6. Past service cost, curtailments and settlements","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Plan changes hit profit or loss at once. ","type":"text"},{"text":"Past service cost","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" is the change in the DBO for service already rendered, caused by a plan amendment (introducing or changing a plan) or a ","type":"text"},{"text":"curtailment","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (a significant reduction in the number of employees covered). It is recognised as an expense at the earlier of when the amendment or curtailment occurs and when related restructuring costs or termination benefits are recognised (para. 103). For example (illustration), a subsidiary raises a lump-sum benefit by 10 percent for a group whose DBO is 50,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Past service cost = 50,000 × 10% = 5,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Employee benefits expense 5,000 / Cr Net defined benefit liability 5,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Since the 2011 revision of IAS 19 (in Indonesia from 2015) past service cost is no longer spread over the vesting period.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A ","type":"text"},{"text":"settlement","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" removes the obligation altogether, for example when the fund buys annuities from an insurer that takes over the payments. The gain or loss is the difference between the DBO settled and the price paid. For example (illustration), the fund pays an insurer 41,500 to take over pensions in payment with a DBO of 40,000.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Settlement loss = 41,500 − 40,000 = 1,500","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Memo: DBO −40,000; plan assets −41,500","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Employee benefits expense 1,500 / Cr Net defined benefit liability 1,500","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A purchased policy settles the plan only if the insurer, not the employer, becomes obliged to the pensioners.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7. Indonesian severance as a defined benefit plan","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Indonesian labour law promises every permanent employee a lump sum at retirement set by a formula, so the employer carries a DB plan under PSAK 219, unfunded unless it sets assets aside. The formula sits in UU 13/2003 as amended by the Job Creation law (UU 11/2020, replaced by Perpu 2/2022 and enacted as UU 6/2023), and in its implementing regulation PP 35/2021. On retirement an employee receives (PP 35/2021, art. 56):","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"severance pay (","type":"text"},{"text":"uang pesangon","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":") of 1.75 times the art. 40(2) scale, which rises from 1 month's wages for under one year of service to a maximum of 9 months from 8 years;","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"a long-service payment (","type":"text"},{"text":"uang penghargaan masa kerja","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", UPMK) of 1 times the art. 40(3) scale, from 2 months at 3 years to a maximum of 10 months from 24 years;","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"compensation for rights (","type":"text"},{"text":"uang penggantian hak","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"), such as unused leave.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Maximum on retirement = 1.75 × 9 + 10 = 25.75 months' wages, reached at 24 years of service","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Employers pay the higher of the statutory amount and their own plan or collective labour agreement, as PHE's policy says (Note 3q, Daftar 5/63, PDF p. 80). Since Constitutional Court decision 168/PUU-XXI/2023 (31 October 2024) the statutory scale is a minimum (","type":"text"},{"text":"paling sedikit","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"), and the court ordered a new labour law, so these multiples may change.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"When the benefit is earned.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" For years many Indonesian actuaries attributed this benefit straight-line from the date of hire. In an agenda decision published in May 2021 the IFRS Interpretations Committee considered a plan that pays only to those employed at retirement age, capped at a number of years of consecutive service immediately before retirement, and concluded that the benefit is attributed only to the capped years before retirement age, since service before then does not reduce the service still needed. In April 2022 the DSAK IAI, the Indonesian standard setter, found that the statutory scheme has the same fact pattern. With retirement at 56 and the UPMK capped at 24 years, the obligation first arises at age 32, and the benefit is attributed to service between 32 and 56. Entities that had attributed from hire were told to assess the effect under the PSAK 25 (now PSAK 208) requirements for a change in accounting policy.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration), an employee joined at 25, is now 40 with 15 years of service, and retires at 56 with 31. Current monthly wage is 1.5 (USD 1,500), with PHE's 7 percent salary increase and 7.1 percent discount rate, and no resignations or deaths.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Final monthly wage = 1.5 × 1.07^16 = 4.4282; benefit = 25.75 × 4.4282 = 114.027","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Present value of the full benefit = 114.027 / 1.071^16 = 38.052","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Attributed from hire (age 25–56)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Attributed from age 32 (2022 clarification)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Attribution period","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 years","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"24 years","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Years earned at age 40","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"15","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"DBO","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"38.052 × 15 / 31 = 18.412","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"38.052 × 8 / 24 = 12.684","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Benefit earned per year","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"114.027 / 31 = 3.678","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"114.027 / 24 = 4.751","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The later start cuts this employee's DBO by 5.728, or 31.1 percent, and raises the annual unit by 29.2 percent (31/24), so the cost moves later in the career. An employee hired at 35 would see no change, because attribution starts at hire. The younger the hiring age across a workforce, the larger the fall in the opening liability.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Funding it.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" PP 35/2021 art. 58 lets employer contributions to a pension programme count towards statutory severance, with the employer paying any shortfall, so a fund held for that purpose is a plan asset of the severance DB plan and does not turn it into a DC plan. PHE funds its service award (PAP, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Penghargaan Atas Pengabdian","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":") through such a DPLK programme (PPUKP) and treats its assets as plan assets (Note 25b, Daftar 5/120, PDF p. 137). All its other benefits are unfunded.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"8. Other long-term and termination benefits","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Other long-term benefits","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" are measured with the same PUCM, but every element, remeasurements included, goes to profit or loss, because they are less uncertain than post-employment benefits (paras. 154–156). PHE's are long-service awards (","type":"text"},{"text":"Ulang Tahun Dinas","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", UTD), in cash or goods, and the pre-retirement preparation period (MPPK) (Note 3q(iv), Daftar 5/65, PDF p. 82). Its gold price and gold inflation assumptions suggest some awards are paid in gold.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"For example (illustration, in Rp million), an award of 10 grams of gold at 25 years of service, earned evenly. An employee has 22 years of service. Take PHE's 2024 gold price of Rp1,462,500 per gram, gold inflation of 8 percent and the 7.1 percent discount rate.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Award value in 3 years = 10 × 1.4625 × 1.08^3 = Rp18.423 million","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Obligation = 18.423 × 22 / 25 / 1.071^3 = Rp13.197 million","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A 10 percent rise in the gold price adds about Rp1.320 million to the obligation, charged straight to profit or loss.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Termination benefits","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" arise from the decision to end employment, not from service (para. 159). Only the extra paid to induce early leaving is a termination benefit. It is recognised at the earlier of when the entity can no longer withdraw the offer and when it recognises related restructuring costs under PSAK 237 (para. 165). For example (illustration), 120 employees accept a voluntary early-retirement package of 30 each, and each already carries a recognised DBO of 20.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Package = 120 × 30 = 3,600; DBO settled = 120 × 20 = 2,400; termination benefit = 3,600 − 2,400 = 1,200","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Net defined benefit liability 2,400, Dr Termination benefits expense 1,200 / Cr Cash 3,600","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9. Employee benefits across industries","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"State-owned groups and banks with their own pension funds.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" Many run employer pension funds (","type":"text"},{"text":"Dana Pensiun Pemberi Kerja","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", DPPK) offering final-salary DB pensions alongside statutory severance. With large plan assets, a fall in bond yields or in asset returns can swing equity through OCI. Closing a DB plan to future accrual and moving staff to DC is a plan amendment, and any fall in the DBO, for example from removing the final-salary link, is a negative past service cost in profit or loss.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Labour-intensive industries.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" In plantations, textiles, footwear and manufacturing the unfunded severance obligation can be large relative to equity, and the 2022 attribution change mattered most where staff are hired young. In-kind benefits such as plantation workers' rice and housing (","type":"text"},{"text":"natura","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":") are short-term benefits at cost.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Pension funds, insurers and share-based pay.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" A DPPK reports its own statements under PSAK 226 (formerly PSAK 18, IAS 26), an insurer carries annuities bought to settle a plan under PSAK 117 (formerly PSAK 74, IFRS 17), and share-based pay follows PSAK 102 (IFRS 2).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Upstream oil and gas.","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"text":" An operator employs a contract area's staff on behalf of all partners, who carry their shares of benefit costs through joint billings, and under a cost recovery production sharing contract (PSC) those costs are generally recoverable. PHE notes that such operators provide post-employment benefits to their own permanent staff (Note 3q, PDF p. 81).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"10. Presentation and analysis: PHE's Note 25","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE gives most of the disclosures PSAK 219 asks for: plan descriptions, reconciliations, the asset mix, assumptions, sensitivities and the timing of payments (Note 25b, Daftar 5/119–5/124, PDF pp. 136–141).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null}}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2023","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Post-employment, direct hire","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"221,421","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"237,309","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Post-employment, Pertamina secondees","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"46,189","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"50,942","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Other long-term, direct hire","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"38,530","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"9,407","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Other long-term, secondees","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,871","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"20,946","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Employee benefits liabilities, non-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"310,011","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"318,604","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The funded status reconciles to the line only after one step.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"DBO 657,210 − plan assets 387,381 = deficit 269,829; + asset ceiling 1,652 = 271,481","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"271,481 + other long-term, direct hire 38,530 = 310,011 (2023: 309,197 + 9,407 = 318,604)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The 271,481 is 3,871 more than the post-employment total of 267,610, exactly the secondees' other long-term benefits (2023: 20,946). The funded-status table and the DBO movement therefore appear to include them, and the post-employment liability movement (267,610) does not. PHE does not say so.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Funded ratio = 387,381 / 657,210 = 58.9% (2023: 329,152 / 635,792 = 51.8%)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Plan assets: money market 80%, debt instruments 8%, equity 6%, investment funds 6% (2023: 57%, 29%, 8%, 6%)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Only PAP is funded, so the ratio mixes one funded plan with unfunded ones. It rose on contributions of 74,643 against benefits of 31,410, and the fund moved into money market instruments. Undiscounted expected payments total 1,777,541, 2.7 times the DBO.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The sensitivity table gives a DBO of 526,700 at a discount rate 1 point higher and 637,468 at 1 point lower. An approximate ","type":"text"},{"text":"duration","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", the percentage change in the DBO per point of rate, is (637,468 − 526,700) / (2 × 0.01 × 582,084) = 9.5 years, where 582,084 is the midpoint. On the reported DBO it would be 8.4 years. Both figures lie below that DBO, as in 2023, so they were probably run on a narrower population or an earlier date. PHE does not disclose the weighted average duration that para. 147(c) requires.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The OCI remeasurement appears as four different figures across the statements and Note 25, and interest income, the return on plan assets, contributions, benefits and the ceiling change also differ between tables (see Least-confident). PHE's policy still describes the pre-2015 rules for past service cost and curtailment (Note 3q(iii), PDF pp. 81–82).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Deferred tax on these liabilities totals 8,711 + 27,119 = 35,830, with 161 charged to OCI in 2024 (Note 43e, Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"11. Upstream note: secondees from Pertamina","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Part of PHE's workforce are Pertamina employees seconded to the group. Since 2020 Pertamina's policy has required each subsidiary to recognise its share of six benefits Pertamina owes those secondees (the Pertamina pension fund DPP, PAP, retiree healthcare, repatriation, MPPK and UTD) for the period they serve there (Note 25, Daftar 5/118, PDF p. 135). Before 2020 subsidiaries were charged only an allocated expense. A DB plan that shares risk between entities under common control is not a multi-employer plan, and each entity recognises the cost charged to it under the group's agreement or policy (paras. 40–41). PHE carries the secondees' share in its DBO (46,189 post-employment at the end of 2024), and its expense table shows a separate \"PT Pertamina (Persero) allocation\" of 5,108 (2023: 11,658), presumably the service cost Pertamina charges under Note 3q; the FS do not say.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"12. Related lines","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"short-term-employee-benefits","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"text":": the incentives payable in section 2.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"oci-remeasurement-employee-benefits","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}]},{"text":": the remeasurements in section 5.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"production-expenses","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/production-expenses","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"text":"general-administrative-expenses","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/production-expenses","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"text":"expense-from-other-operations","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/production-expenses","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": where service cost and net interest are charged.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"deferred-tax-assets","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"text":"deferred-tax-liabilities","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/deferred-tax-assets","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": the temporary differences on these liabilities.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"other-taxes-payable","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"rel":"noopener noreferrer nofollow","href":"/accounting/fsli/other-taxes-receivable-current","class":"text-primary underline cursor-pointer hover:text-primary/80","title":null,"target":"_blank"}}]},{"text":": withholding tax on salaries.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Least-confident assertions","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"That PHE's funded-status table and DBO movement include secondees' other long-term benefits is inferred from the 3,871 and 20,946 differences matching to the unit in both years. Direct-hire and secondee long-term balances also swapped size between years (9,407 → 38,530; 20,946 → 3,871), which may mean relabelling, so the label is less certain than the arithmetic. The English text of Note 25b dates the actuarial calculation to 2023 and the Indonesian to 2024; this page follows the Indonesian.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PHE's notes disagree in detail. The OCI remeasurement is −610 in the statement (labelled a gain, possibly net of the 161 of deferred tax), 13,584 in Note 25's OCI table (labelled gains although the salary assumption rose), +11,397 in the DBO movement and +9,566 in the net liability movement. Interest income on plan assets is 22,333 and 20,760; the return beyond interest appears as −535, −241 (in profit or loss, against para. 127 and PHE's policy) and 1,903; contributions are 74,643 and 74,360; benefits reduce the DBO by 30,400 and plan assets by 31,410, although only PAP is funded; the asset ceiling fell by 905 but the OCI table shows 304. The 2023 other long-term movement puts 2,269 in OCI against policy. Note 4 gives the carrying amount as US$30,271 (2023: US$125,528) in Indonesian and US$125,528 for both years in English (Daftar 5/85, PDF p. 102). MPPK is other long-term in Note 3q(iv) but listed under post-employment in Note 25b.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The sensitivity figures sit below the reported DBO at both rates in both years, so the 9.5-year (8.4 on the reported DBO) duration is indicative only. That the 5,108 allocation is Pertamina's service-cost charge is a reading; the FS do not say.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"That some UTD awards are paid in gold is read from the gold assumptions; the FS do not say. The industry paragraphs (DPPK closures, plantation natura, the size of the 2022 effect on labour-intensive firms) are general practice, not researched for named companies. The treatment of PSC cost recovery of benefit costs is stated generally; PHE's FS do not describe it for employee benefits.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The severance example ignores resignation, death and disability, which an actuary would weight in, although the statutory formula also sets those benefits, and ignores ","type":"text"},{"text":"uang penggantian hak","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":". After decision 168/PUU-XXI/2023 a new labour law was ordered; its status at the date of this page was not checked, and it may change the multiples.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"References","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PT Pertamina Hulu Energi dan Entitas Anak, consolidated financial statements 31 December 2024 (audited, USD thousand): statement of financial position (Daftar 1/2, PDF p. 12); statement of profit or loss and OCI (Daftar 2/1, PDF p. 14); Note 3q (Daftar 5/63–5/65, PDF pp. 80–82); Note 4 (Daftar 5/85, PDF p. 102); Note 25 (Daftar 5/118–5/124, PDF pp. 135–141); Notes 37–39 (Daftar 5/149, PDF p. 166); Note 43e (Daftar 5/155, PDF p. 172). Indonesian and English columns both read. VERIFIED (PHE FS 2024, on disk).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The page follows the order of Kieso, Weygandt and Warfield, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Intermediate Accounting","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", IFRS edition, chs. 13 and 20, and the structure of PSAK 219. The book was not opened for this page. TEXTBOOK — NOT OPENED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 219 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Imbalan Kerja","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IAS 19 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Employee Benefits","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", 2011), paras. 8, 13–16, 19, 40–41, 64, 67, 70–73, 83, 103–105, 120, 122–123, 127, 147(c), 153–156, 159–160, 165. VERIFIED (web, IAS 19 as adopted by Commission Regulation (EU) No 475/2012, eur-lex.europa.eu; paras. 104–105, 127, 147(c) confirmed in the independent recheck). Paras. 28 and 30 (DC and DB definitions), 135–147 (disclosure) and the 2015 Indonesian effective date of the 2011 revision: STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"DSAK IAI, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Siaran Pers – Pengatribusian Imbalan pada Periode Jasa","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", April 2022, citing IFRS Interpretations Committee agenda decision \"Attributing Benefit to Periods of Service (IAS 19)\", May 2021. VERIFIED (web, iaiglobal.or.id PDF, read in full).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PP 35/2021, arts. 40(2)–(4), 56 and 58. VERIFIED (web, secondary: BP Lawyers; detik.com quoting art. 56; hukumonline.com and Bisnis.com on art. 58). Constitutional Court decision 168/PUU-XXI/2023 (31 October 2024), art. 156(2) read as ","type":"text"},{"text":"paling sedikit","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":": VERIFIED (web, secondary: hukumonline.com, \"Putusan MK Ini Buka Peluang Buruh Terima Pesangon Lebih Tinggi\"); the court's text not opened. UU 6/2023 enacting Perpu 2/2022 and revoking UU 11/2020: VERIFIED (web, peraturan.bpk.go.id).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 226 (IAS 26, formerly PSAK 18): title VERIFIED (web, secondary: Binus Accounting renumbering table). PSAK 102 (IFRS 2), PSAK 117 (IFRS 17, formerly PSAK 74), PSAK 208 (IAS 8, formerly PSAK 25), PSAK 212 (IAS 12), PSAK 237 (IAS 37): numbers per IAI renumbering rule; content STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb,
       presentation = $p${"deck":"The theory of employee benefit accounting under PSAK 219 from payroll accruals to the projected unit credit method, worked through PHE's pension and severance obligations and set beside the industries where these liabilities weigh most","genre":null,"author_bio":null,"references":[],"hero_caption":null,"key_takeaways":["PSAK 219 sorts employee benefits by when they are paid. Short-term benefits are accrued undiscounted as the work is done; post-employment, other long-term and termination benefits are estimated, discounted, and recognised on their own triggers.","A defined contribution plan costs the employer only its contribution. A defined benefit plan leaves the employer holding the actuarial and investment risk, so it carries the present value of the promised benefit, measured by the projected unit credit method, less any plan assets.","Service cost and net interest on a defined benefit plan go to profit or loss, while remeasurements (actuarial gains and losses, the return on plan assets beyond interest, and changes in the asset ceiling) go to other comprehensive income and are never recycled. Indonesian statutory severance is an unfunded defined benefit plan of this kind, and since 2022 it is attributed only to the last 24 years of service before retirement age, or from hire if later."],"revision_note":null}$p$::jsonb,
       updated_at = now()
